var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Reading-and-writing-with-Inscription.jl","page":"Usage","text":"Reading and writing happens fundamentally via encoding into and decoding from MemoryViews.jl's MemoryView type. Loading from and storing to BufferIO.jl's AbstractBufReader and AbstractBufWriter works via encoding and decoding from memory views into their buffers.\n\nWhen loading and storing from BufferIO I/O types, you can always manually request their buffer, encode into/decode from the buffer, then call BufferIO.consume. This can be used as an escape hatch to load and store types not easily handled by Inscription.jl, such as bit-packed types (see the Implementing a type page). It can also be used in extremely performance-sensitive situations when you can request a large buffer, do a single boundscheck, then load many values inbounds. However, most users should just use the Inscription functions below, as they are usually fast enough, and cover most use cases.","title":"Reading and writing with Inscription.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Encoding-and-decoding","page":"Usage","text":"Encoding and decoding here means reading from / writing to MemoryViews, whereas loading and storing refers to reading from / writing to BufferIO types.\n\nThe functions mentioned below: encode!, decode, decode_exact and decode_next each have try_* versions. The plain functions throw exceptions on errors, whereas the try_ versions return the exception instead. On the happy path, the throwing versions are slightly more efficient, so use the try_ versions only if you plan to actually handle errors.","title":"Encoding and decoding"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Decoding-values","page":"Usage","text":"The decoding signatures are generally: (try_)decode(_exact,_next)(mem, [i::Integer,] ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding), and they mean: Decode a T from mem starting at position i, with the given Encoding, and return the T (or an error, for the try_ variant). Like the encoding functions, mem is converted to an ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}, and i defaults to 1 if omitted.\n\nExample: decode(UInt8[1,2,3,4], UInt32, LittleEndian()) === 0x04030201\n\nThe function decode_next returns Tuple{T, Int} (or an error, for the try_ variant), with the Int giving the index immediately after the T, in mem. This way, you know how many bytes were read. This is useful if the T is stored in a variable number of bytes, for example a Char in the UTF8 encoding.\n\nThe function decode_exact throws an error if mem contains any trailing data. This can be used to ensure mem contains exactly the T. E.g. a hypothetical decimal integer decoder would fail on the input memory \"123abc\", as \"abc\" is not a valid decimal integer, whereas the normal decode would read the three bytes and return 123.","title":"Decoding values"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Encoding-values","page":"Usage","text":"Encoding functions generally have the signature (try_)encode!(mem, [i::Integer], x, ::Encoding), and they mean: Encode x into mem starting at position i with the given Encoding, and return how many bytes were written (or an error, for the try_ variant). The argument mem is converted to a MutableMemoryView{UInt8}. If i is omitted, it defaults to 1.\n\nExample: encode!(zeros(UInt8, 4), UInt32(55), LittleEndian()) === 4\n\nTo find the number of bytes a value x takes up in some encoding, encode it: try_encode! returns the number of bytes written.","title":"Encoding values"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Bulk-encoding-and-decoding","page":"Usage","text":"When encoding or decoding multiple values together, it can be more efficient to encode or decode entire arrays of values in bulk. For trivial codecs, this may be compiled to a memmove, or be vectorized.\n\nencode_every!(dst, src, ::Encoding)::Int constructs a MutableMemoryView{UInt8} from dst, an ImmutableMemoryView{T} from src, and then encodes all values from src to dst, returning the number of bytes written. If dst cannot hold all values, throw a bounds error. For other errors, throw an EncodeError.\n\nThe function decode_every! is analogous, except returning the number of bytes read, and throwing a DecodeError on insufficient input, or an invalid encoding.\n\nIf you don't want to handle exceptions, encode_all! and decode_all! stop at the first error, and return (n_elements_encoded, n_bytes_written) and (n_elements_decoded, n_bytes_read), respectively. These bulk functions have no try_ equivalents: To see the error that stopped them, use try_encode! or try_decode at the first index after the bytes used.","title":"Bulk encoding and decoding"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Loading-and-storing","page":"Usage","text":"As mentioned above, you can use the functions below to load and store directly, without manually handling buffers.\n\nload(::AbstractBufReader, ::Type{T}, ::Encoding) and store(::AbstractBufWriter, x::T, ::Encoding) are similar to encoding and decoding, except that they load from and store to the reader and writer, respectively, and advance their state (i.e. call consume internally). These loads and stores are all or nothing, not in the sense of promising to be single atomic operations on a CPU level, but in that, if the operation fails, no bytes are read from, or written to, the I/O object.\n\nThe above throwing functions also have try_load and try_store equivalents. Like the encoding/decoding functions, they are slightly less efficient.\n\nIn the rare case the stream you read from is not self-synchronizing - that is, it's not clear from the data where the value ends, load_exact(::AbstractBufReader, n::Integer, ::Type{T}, ::Encoding) and try_load_exact read a T from exactly n bytes of the reader. Consider a hypothetical Decimal integer codec where the string \"123\" can be read as (1, 2, 3), (12, 3), (1, 23), or 123. Using load or try_load, the maximal length would be read, so 123 would be returned. To load (12, 3) instead, use load_exact(io, 2, Int, Decimal()) then load_exact(io, 1, Int, Decimal()).","title":"Loading and storing"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Bulk-loading-and-storing","page":"Usage","text":"These functions internally use the bulk encoding and decoding functions, and so may be more efficient, for example when filling arrays.\n\nThe function load_into!(::AbstractBufReader, dst, ::Encoding) builds a MutableMemoryView{T} from dst, then reads data of type T into dst, and returns (n_elements_loaded, n_bytes_read). It stops, without throwing, if dst has no more space, the reader is at EOF, or the next element cannot be decoded. Like the bulk encoding/decoding functions, it has no try_ equivalent. To see why it stopped, check the length of dst, check whether the reader is at EOF, and call try_load to see the error.\n\nThe number of elements loaded is decided by the implementation of the reader. The reader knows the optimal buffer size, so delegating the amount read per call to the reader improves performance. However, to guarantee forward progress, this function loads zero elements only if it is stopped by one of the above reasons, i.e. the reader cannot decide a buffer size of zero.\n\nUse this function when you process a larger array in chunks, but have no strong opinion on the chunk size yourself. For example, a hashing function might need to process a large byte array, but operate on chunks of 8 UInt64 values per round. Here, you could call load_into! in a loop until at least 8 elements have been loaded (which, in the vast majority of cases, will happen after a single call), then process that chunk.\n\nThe function store_into is the mirror image: It writes to an AbstractBufWriter, reads from src, and returns (n_elements_read, n_bytes_written). Like load_into!, the number of elements is an implementation detail (but nonzero, unless prevented), and there is no try_ equivalent.\n\nThe functions load_every! and store_every are similar to load_into! and store_into, but continue until dst is filled, or src is exhausted. If an element cannot be decoded or encoded, or there is no room in the writer, or there are not enough bytes in the reader, throw an exception. That is, these functions use the entire array passed to it instead of delegating choice of buffer size to the reader/writer type. Use these functions if you need to process the entire array before continuing.\n\nFinally, load_all! and store_all are similar to load_every! and store_every, but stop early instead of throwing when an element cannot be encoded/decoded, or there are not enough bytes in the reader or room in the writer. You can then use try_load and try_store to get the error.","title":"Bulk loading and storing"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Boundschecks-when-loading-and-storing","page":"Usage","text":"Unlike the encode and decode functions, load and store functions do not respect @inbounds (they always boundscheck). This may be undesirable when reading many small chunks of data, where optimal performance is needed. In these cases, consider getting a buffer, then decoding with @inbounds, then calling consume manually.\n\nFor example, to read 3 UInt64 from a reader, you may do:\n\nusing BufferIO: AbstractBufReader, get_nonempty_buffer, consume\nusing Inscription: Inscription, decode, LittleEndian, DecodeError\nusing Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds: EndOfInput\n\nfunction get_three_u64(io::AbstractBufReader)\n    # Throw a DecodeError if EOF is reached before 24 bytes\n    buffer = @something get_nonempty_buffer(io, 24) throw(DecodeError(EndOfInput))\n    # For methods generic over AbstractBufReader, guard against\n    # invalid implementations of get_nonempty_buffer, like here:\n    length(buffer) < 24 && error(\"Invalid implementation of get_nonempty_buffer\")\n    @inbounds begin\n        values = (\n            decode(buffer,  1, UInt64, LittleEndian()),\n            decode(buffer,  9, UInt64, LittleEndian()),\n            decode(buffer, 17, UInt64, LittleEndian()),\n        )\n    end\n    consume(io, 24)\n    return values\nend\n\nThe above could also be done directly with try_load(io, NTuple{3, UInt64}, LittleEndian()), so the example above is only for demonstration purposes.","title":"Boundschecks when loading and storing"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Endian-convenience-namespaces","page":"Usage","text":"The most common format for reading and writing values are the native binary representation. In Inscription, these are the encodings LittleEndian() and BigEndian(). When working with e.g. exclusively little-endian data, it's annoying to have to pass LittleEndian() as the Encoding to all functions.\n\nInscription provides the modules Inscription.Little and Inscription.Big, which export convenience functions that automatically forward LittleEndian() and BigEndian(), such that e.g.:\n\nusing Inscription.Little: encode!\n\nencode!(mem, UInt32(123))\n\nIs shorthand for:\n\nusing Inscription: encode!\n\nencode!(mem, UInt32(123), LittleEndian())\n\nIn all examples below, I will use the more verbose encode!(mem, UInt32(123), LittleEndian()) for clarity. The full list of functions in these modules is on the Reference page.","title":"Endian convenience namespaces"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Handling-errors","page":"Usage","text":"The try_* functoin may return DecodeError or EncodeError - the same exceptions thrown by the non-try_* functions.\n\nThese errors currently have two public properties: A .kind property, and a .payload property. The .kind is DecodeErrorKind or EncodeErrorKind - enums stating what went wrong. The .payload is Union{Nothing, Some{T}} - nothing unless .kind is InvalidData (for decoding errors) or InvalidValue (for encoding errors).\n\nThis allow codecs to return specific errors saying why a value could not be decoded or encoded. For example, a codec that parses decimal integers may give the payload Some(Overflow), so that you can try again with a wider type. A codec that that gives no reason for its failure will typically gives the payload Some(nothing).","title":"Handling errors"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Little-and-Big","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Little and Big"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#DecodeErrorKinds","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"DecodeErrorKinds"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#EncodeErrorKinds","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"EncodeErrorKinds"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/#Inscription.Inscription","page":"Reference","text":"module Inscription\n\nEncode and decode values, such as integers, floats, Char, structs to and from byte arrays and BufferIO's I/O types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.Inscription"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.BigEndian","page":"Reference","text":"BigEndian() <: Encoding\n\nEncoding signifying copying the raw bytes of the value in big-endian byte order. See Encoding for more details.\n\nSee also: LittleEndian, NATIVE_ENDIAN, Endianness\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.BigEndian"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.BitsLoadable","page":"Reference","text":"BitsLoadable() <: CodecScheme\n\nThe fastest CodecScheme: the encoding in NATIVE_ENDIAN is its in-memory representation.\n\nSee CodecScheme, Encoding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.BitsLoadable"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.BitsStorable","page":"Reference","text":"BitsStorable() <: CodecScheme\n\nThe second-fastest CodecScheme: For valid instances, the encoding in NATIVE_ENDIAN are their in-memory representation.\n\nSee CodecScheme, Encoding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.BitsStorable"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.CodecScheme","page":"Reference","text":"abstract type CodecScheme end\n\nSupertype of zero-sized trait structs that signals if a type / Encoding pair can have auto-derived codec implementations.\n\nFor some types / encoding pairs like Float32 being encoded in BigEndian, or LittleEndian the codec can be automatically implemented, because the codec is simply loading/storing with a pointer, and maybe byte swapping. For such a type T, users can declare:\n\nInscription.codec_scheme(::Type{T}, ::Endianness) = Inscription.BitsLoadable()\n\nAnd then the codec T/Endianness is automatically implemented.\n\nThe fallback for codec_scheme(T, E) is Manual. The current list of opt-in codec schemes beyond Manual are:\n\nBitsLoadable\nBitsStorable\nProxy\n\nSee extended help for which type / encoding pairs are eligible to opt into these codec schemes, and which methods they must implement.\n\nExtended help\n\nA custom type T can opt into the following codec schemes. It should generally opt into the first applicable one on this list, as they are listed from most to least convenient.\n\nSome methods are only needed for either decoding (reading) or encoding (writing). A program that only decodes a type need not implement the methods that are only needed for encoding it, and vice versa. Attempting to encode or decode without the needed methods throws a MethodError.\n\nBitsLoadable\n\nThis codec scheme means T is transparently read/writable using a pointer load/store. BitsLoadable must be declared for the Endianness encodings only. To implement BitsLoadable, type T needs nothing other than to define:\n\ncodec_scheme(::Type{T}, ::Endianness) = BitsLoadable()\n\nA type T can be BitsLoadable if:\n\nisbitstype(T),\nT does not have size zero\nIf T is a struct, all its fields must be BitsLoadable\nT must have no padding on any platform, i.e. is_padding_free(T) must be true.See is_padding_free(T) for details.\nIts NATIVE_ENDIAN encoding is the same as its in-memory representation, i.e. for x::T, the encoding is exactly equal to reinterpret(UInt8, [x]).\nEvery bit pattern is a valid T.\n\nBitsStorable\n\nThis codec scheme means T is transparently writeable using a pointer store, and is readable using a pointer load, except not all bitpatterns are valid, and therefore the bits must be checked before instantiation. BitsStorable must be declared for the Endianness encodings only.\n\nTo implement BitsStorable, type T needs to implement:\n\ncodec_scheme(::Type{T}, ::Endianness) = BitsStorable()\n\n# Only needed for decoding\nvalidate_bits(::Type{T}, ::NTuple{sizeof(T), UInt8})::Union{Nothing, Invalid} = ...\n# Optional, and only used for decoding: the type of the payloads of `validate_bits`\ncodec_decode_invalid_type(::Type{T}, ::Endianness) = ...\n\nBitsStorable has the same restrictions as BitsLoadable, except:\n\nNot all bitpatterns need be valid\nIf it's a struct, all fields must only be recursively BitsLoadable OR BitsStorable.\n\nProxy{P}\n\nThis codec scheme means T can be encoded/decoded via an instance of P. For example, a type struct Foo; x::UInt32; y::UInt8; end may be decoded via a Tuple{UInt32, UInt8}.\n\nTo implement Proxy{P}, type T for encoding E needs to implement:\n\ncodec_scheme(::Type{T}, ::E) = Proxy{P}()\n\n# Only needed for encoding\ntry_to_proxy(::T, ::E)::Union{P, Invalid} = ...\n# Optional, and only used for encoding: the type of the payloads of `try_to_proxy`\ncodec_encode_invalid_type(::Type{T}, ::E) = ...\n\n# Only needed for decoding\ntry_from_proxy(::Type{T}, ::P, ::E)::Union{T, Invalid} = ...\n# Optional, and only used for decoding: the type of the payloads of `try_from_proxy`\ncodec_decode_invalid_type(::Type{T}, ::E) = ...\n\nAny type can be Proxy, but it only makes sense to implement if T can be easily created from / used to create a P, which already has an encoding.\n\nManual\n\nThe fallback codec.\n\nTo implement a Manual codec for type T over encoding E, you should implement:\n\ncodec_max_encoded_size(::Type{T}, ::E)::Union{Val{N}, Unbounded()}\n\n# Only needed for decoding\ncodec_try_decode(::DecodeInput, ::Type{T}, ::E)::Union{Tuple{T, Int}, Invalid, TooShort}\n# Optional, and only used for decoding: the type of the payloads of `codec_try_decode`\ncodec_decode_invalid_type(::Type{T}, ::E) = ...\n\n# Only needed for encoding\ncodec_try_encode!(::EncodeOutput, ::T, ::E)::Union{Int, Invalid, TooShort}\n# Optional, and only used for encoding: the type of the payloads of `codec_try_encode!`\ncodec_encode_invalid_type(::Type{T}, ::E) = ...\n\nThe payload types default to Nothing, see codec_decode_invalid_type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.CodecScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.DecodeError","page":"Reference","text":"DecodeError{P} <: Exception\n\nA failure to decode or load a value, caused by the data. This exception is thrown by the decoding or loading functions such as decode, load, and load_exact, and returned by the try_* functions like try_decode.\n\nIt has the public properties .kind::DecodeErrorKind and .payload::Union{Nothing, Some{P}}.\n\nThe payload is Some(x::P) when the kind is InvalidData, where x is the payload of the Invalid the codec returned. For other kinds, it is nothing.\n\nFor the current possible kinds, see the docs of DecodeErrorKind.\n\nThe type of P is decode_invalid_type(T, enc), and defaults to Nothing.\n\nFunctions prefixed with try_ return a DecodeError instead of throwing it. The functions without the prefix throw the same value.\n\nSee also: DecodeErrorKinds.DecodeErrorKind, @?\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using BufferIO: CursorReader\n\njulia> io = CursorReader([0x01, 0x00, 0x02]);\n\njulia> while true\n           x = try_load(io, UInt16, LittleEndian())\n           if x isa DecodeError\n               x.kind === DecodeErrorKinds.EndOfInput && break\n               println(\"Error: \", x.kind)\n               break\n           end\n           println(x)\n       end\n1\nError: Truncated\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.DecodeError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.DecodeInput","page":"Reference","text":"abstract type DecodeInput\n\nThe input of codec_try_decode: the bytes to decode a value from, at the start of a sequence of bytes. Not all bytes of the sequence may be available yet.\n\nA codec reads from DecodeInput only through these functions:\n\nrequire_bytes(input, n): the first n bytes, as a memory view of length n,\npeek_byte(input, i): the byte at index i, or nothing if the input ends before it,\ndecode_field(input, T, enc): a value of type T from the start, the number of bytes it used, and the input after them.\n\nThese functions may return TooShort when the bytes they need are not available. A codec doesn't handle TooShort itself, but returns it, which can conveniently be done with @?, as in @? peek_byte(input, 1). The caller then decides what TooShort means: it may e.g. report that the input is truncated, or run the codec again once more bytes are available.\n\nCode only against DecodeInput. Its concrete subtypes are internal, and may change in any release.\n\nExtended help\n\nInvalidated views\n\nA read may make more bytes of the input available, which may invalidate previous views from require_bytes. To be correct for all inputs, a codec must follow these rules:\n\nA call to require_bytes, peek_byte or decode_field which reaches beyond all earlier reads may invalidate all previous views. E.g. if the earlier reads reached index 10, reads reaching indices > 10 may invalidate previous views, but not reads reaching only indices < 11.\nAll views are invalidated when codec_try_decode returns. So, do not return views, or otherwise let views escape from codec_try_decode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.DecodeInput"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.EncodeError","page":"Reference","text":"EncodeError{P} <: Exception\n\nA failure to encode or store a value. This exception is thrown by the encoding or storing functions such as encode! and store, and returned by the try_* functions like try_encode!.\n\nIt has the public properties .kind::EncodeErrorKind and .payload::Union{Nothing, Some{P}}.\n\nThe payload is Some(x::P) when the kind is InvalidValue, where x is the payload of the Invalid the codec returned. For other kinds, it is nothing.\n\nFor the current possible kinds, see the docs of EncodeErrorKind.\n\nThe type of P is encode_invalid_type(T, enc), and defaults to Nothing.\n\nFunctions prefixed with try_ return an EncodeError instead of throwing it. The functions without the prefix throw the same value.\n\nSee also: EncodeErrorKinds.EncodeErrorKind, @?\n\nExamples\n\njulia> try_encode!(MemoryView(zeros(UInt8, 3)), 0x01020304, BigEndian())\nEncodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds.InsufficientSpace)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.EncodeError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.EncodeOutput","page":"Reference","text":"EncodeOutput\n\nThe output of codec_try_encode!: the bytes to encode a value into. A codec writes to EncodeOutput only through the following functions:\n\nreserve_bytes(output, n): the first n bytes, as a mutable memory view of length n, for the codec to write;\nencode_field(output, x, enc): encode x at the start, and return the number of bytes written, and the output after them.\n\nThese functions may return TooShort, which can conveniently be returned early with @?, as in @? reserve_bytes(output, 10).\n\nCode only against EncodeOutput. Its type parameters and fields are internal, and may change in any release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.EncodeOutput"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.Encoding","page":"Reference","text":"abstract type Encoding end\n\nEncoding are zero-sized trait structs used to signal how one value of a given type T is encoded to a given encoding, i.e. sequence of bytes, and decoded from the encodings.\n\nThus, reading and writing behaviour is governed by the pair (T, enc::Encoding). Such an implementation over a pair is called a \"codec\". Inscription's functions like load, store_all, decode_every, etc. use the codecs as their inner kernel.\n\nUsers are free to define their own Encodings and implement codecs for them.\n\nTwo Encodings are special: LittleEndian and BigEndian, together referred to as Endian Encodings (Endianness). Conceptually, these signal loading and storing the raw bits of the value of T. For example, encoding the UInt32 0x01020304 in LittleEndian encodes it to the following bytes [0x04, 0x03, 0x02, 0x01].\n\nCodecs for Endian Encodings can be automatically implemented by declaring the type BitsLoadable or BitsStorable, and a type can be encoded as another type with any encoding by declaring a Proxy. See CodecScheme.\n\nSee also: Endianness, CodecScheme\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.Encoding"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.Invalid","page":"Reference","text":"Invalid{P}(payload::P)\nInvalid(payload)::Invalid{typeof(payload)}\nInvalid()\n\nReturned by codec_try_decode when the input is not a valid encoding, or by codec_try_encode! when the input does not have an encoding.\n\nThe payload is of type P, which is  codec_decode_invalid_type(T, E()) when decoding, or codec_encode_invalid_type(T, E()) when encoding. It stores a more detailed reason why the input was invalid.\n\nFor codecs who don't want to provide such information, leave P to its default of Nothing, and return Invalid(), which is short for Invalid{Nothing}(nothing).\n\nThe user may return an Invalid{Q} where Q <: P from codec_try_decode or codec_try_encode!, and this will be automatically converted to Invalid{P}. E.g. if codec_decode_invalid_type returns Union{Int, Nothing}, you can return Invalid() from codec_try_decode.\n\nSee also: TooShort\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.Invalid"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.LittleEndian","page":"Reference","text":"LittleEndian() <: Encoding\n\nEncoding signifying copying the raw bytes of the value in little-endian byte order. See Encoding for more details.\n\nSee also: BigEndian, NATIVE_ENDIAN, Endianness\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.LittleEndian"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.Manual","page":"Reference","text":"Manual() <: CodecScheme\n\nThe default CodecScheme: the codec of a type with an encoding is implemented by hand.\n\nSee CodecScheme, Encoding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.Manual"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.Proxy","page":"Reference","text":"Proxy{P}() <: CodecScheme\n\nCodecScheme of a type T that is encoded exactly as a value of the concrete type P is, with the same encoding.\n\nSee CodecScheme, Encoding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.Proxy"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.TooShort","page":"Reference","text":"TooShort\n\nReturned by the functions that a codec reads or writes bytes through, when the input or output is too short, and returned by the codec with @?. See DecodeInput and EncodeOutput. A codec does not construct it itself.\n\nWhen decoding, TooShort may also mean that more bytes are needed to decide the result than are available yet. A codec never returns it when its input or output has max_encoded_size bytes.\n\nSee also: Invalid\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.TooShort"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.UTF8","page":"Reference","text":"UTF8() <: Encoding\n\nUTF-8 encoding, e.g. of Char.\n\nEncoding and decoding follows the iteration of Base.String. In details, the rules are as follows:\n\nFor decoding, the rules are:\n\nA lead byte c0-df, e0-ef or f0-f7 starts a Char of at most 2, 3 or 4 bytes, respectively. The following bytes are part of the Char while they are continuation bytes (80-bf), up to that length.\nAny other byte (ASCII, a continuation byte, or f8-ff) is decoded alone as a Char.\nThe bytes are not validated further, so the decoded Char may be malformed or invalid. For example, [0xe4, 0xb8, 0x30] decodes as '\\xe4\\xb8' followed by '0', and the overlong [0xc0, 0x80] as the single Char '\\xc0\\x80'.\nIf the input ends partway through a Char, as in [0xc3], the bytes so far are decoded as a malformed Char.\n\nFor encoding, the codeunits are written in UTF8 form, including for invalid UTF8, with the following notable behaviour:\n\nA Char which could never be decoded according to the above rules, such as reinterpret(Char, typemax(UInt32)), can not be encoded and will give an  EncodeError of kind InvalidValue.\nThe concatenated encoding of multiple invalid Chars may be decoded as one valid Char. E.g. the concatenated encoding of '\\xc3' followed by '\\xb8' is [0xc3, 0xb8], which decodes as 'ø'.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> encode!(MemoryView(zeros(UInt8, 4)), 'é', UTF8())\n2\n\njulia> decode_next(MemoryView(\"€!\"), 1, Char, UTF8())\n('€', 4)\n\njulia> decode(MemoryView(b\"\\xe2\\x82\"), Char, UTF8()) # truncated input: a malformed Char\n'\\xe2\\x82': Malformed UTF-8 (category Ma: Malformed, bad data)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.UTF8"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.Unbounded","page":"Reference","text":"Unbounded()\n\nMarker struct that signals an encoding has no, or a large, upper limit on number of encoded bytes, e.g. for null-terminated strings.\n\nThis signals that when loading from an AbstractBufReader, Inscription should not attempt to buffer the maximal size up front. Implemented as codec_max_encoded_size(::Type{T}, ::SomeEncoding) = Unbounded(), with the encoding typically being Endianness.\n\nAlso returned by max_encoded_size.\n\nSee also: codec_max_encoded_size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.Unbounded"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.Endianness","page":"Reference","text":"Endianness = Union{LittleEndian, BigEndian}\n\nSee also: Encoding LittleEndian, BigEndian NATIVE_ENDIAN ```\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.Endianness"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#Inscription.NATIVE_ENDIAN","page":"Reference","text":"NATIVE_ENDIAN::Endianness\n\nThe byte order of the host, either LittleEndian() or BigEndian(). As a rule of thumb, avoid implementing codecs for this constant, since it makes the implementation platform-dependent in surprising ways. Implement a codec for LittleEndian or BigEndian\n\nSee also: Encoding LittleEndian, BigEndian Endianness\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.NATIVE_ENDIAN"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/#Inscription.@?-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"@? expr\n\nExpands to the equivalent of the following, except that the variable is an anonymous local variable and so cannot clash with any variable from the enclosing scope:\n\nx = expr\nif x isa Union{DecodeError, EncodeError, TooShort, Invalid}\n    return x\nelse\n    x\nend\n\nExamples\n\njulia> function load_pair(io)\n           a = @? try_load(io, UInt16, BigEndian())\n           b = @? try_load(io, UInt16, BigEndian())\n           return (a, b)\n       end;\n\njulia> load_pair(BufferIO.CursorReader([0x00, 0x01, 0x00, 0x02]))\n(0x0001, 0x0002)\n\njulia> load_pair(BufferIO.CursorReader([0x00, 0x01, 0x00]))\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.Truncated)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.@?"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.as_unsigned-Union{Tuple{NTuple{N, UInt8}}, Tuple{N}} where N","page":"Reference","text":"as_unsigned(bytes::NTuple{N, UInt8})::Unsigned\n\nConvert bytes, to the smallest of UInt8, UInt16, UInt32, UInt64 and UInt128 that has at least N bytes, zero-extended. Throw an ArgumentError if N > 16.\n\nThis is a helper function that users can call in validate_bits, if they find it easier to operate on an Unsigned value rather than an NTuple{N, UInt8}.\n\nThe input bytes is given in native byte order. So, for example, if N == 8, this function is equivalent to reinterpret(UInt64, bytes). When used inside validate_bits, the byte order has already been made native, so users need not handle endianness.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> x = 0x00ab_cdef;\n\njulia> bytes = NATIVE_ENDIAN === LittleEndian() ? (0xef, 0xcd, 0xab) : (0xab, 0xcd, 0xef);\n\njulia> Inscription.as_unsigned(bytes) === x\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.as_unsigned"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.codec_decode_invalid_type-Tuple{Type, Encoding}","page":"Reference","text":"codec_decode_invalid_type(::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Type\n\nThe type of the payloads of the Invalids that decoding a T with enc returns, when the data is invalid. Defaults to Nothing, the type of Invalid().\n\nAdd a method to it to give the errors of your codec payloads, e.g. for an enum ParseError:\n\nInscription.codec_decode_invalid_type(::Type{MyInt}, ::Decimal) = ParseError\n\nReturn Union{} if decoding can't fail. The type is checked when the codec returns an Invalid{Q}, and an ErrorException is thrown unless Q <: this type. Since Invalid() is an Invalid{Nothing}, a codec can only return it if the type includes Nothing, e.g. Union{Nothing, ParseError}. Like codec_scheme, its methods must be constant-foldable, and may be defined for abstract encoding types. It is only used for decoding, so a type that is only encoded need not implement it, even if it has payloads when it is encoded. Those are declared with codec_encode_invalid_type.\n\nIt is the type of the payloads that are returned by:\n\ncodec_try_decode of a hand-written codec,\nvalidate_bits of a BitsStorable type,\ntry_from_proxy of a Proxy.\n\nThe payloads of other values decoded as part of a T are passed on: those of the fields of a BitsStorable struct, and of the proxy type of a Proxy. So the payloads of the errors of decoding a T may be of more types than this, see decode_invalid_type. A hand-written codec that decodes other values with decode_field must also declare the types of their payloads, or return its own Invalid instead of theirs.\n\nSee also: codec_encode_invalid_type\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.codec_decode_invalid_type"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.codec_encode_invalid_type-Tuple{Type, Encoding}","page":"Reference","text":"codec_encode_invalid_type(::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Type\n\nThe type of the payloads of the Invalids that encoding a T with enc returns, when the value can't be encoded. Defaults to Nothing, the type of Invalid().\n\nIt is the type of the payloads that are returned by:\n\ncodec_try_encode! of a hand-written codec,\ntry_to_proxy of a Proxy.\n\nLike for codec_decode_invalid_type, the payloads of the proxy type of a Proxy are passed on, see encode_invalid_type. It is only used for encoding, so a type that is only decoded need not implement it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.codec_encode_invalid_type"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.codec_max_encoded_size-Tuple{Type, Encoding}","page":"Reference","text":"codec_max_encoded_size(::Type{T}, ::E)::Union{Val{N}, Unbounded}\n\nExtension point of a hand-written codec for the value type T and the concrete encoding type E, see Encoding. Never call it directly; call max_encoded_size.\n\nReturn Val(N), where N::Int is the maximum number of bytes of any encoding of a T. N must be positive, since every encoding takes at least one byte. It must be a constant, since codecs are compiled for inputs and outputs of N bytes, see DecodeInput. If the encodings have no maximal size, return Unbounded().\n\nStoring into AbstractBufWriter buffers this many bytes before encoding. The input of codec_try_decode ends after this many bytes, also if more bytes follow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.codec_max_encoded_size"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.codec_scheme-Tuple{Type, Encoding}","page":"Reference","text":"codec_scheme(::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::CodecScheme\n\nThe CodecScheme of the type T with the encoding enc. Defaults to Manual().\n\nAdd methods to it for your own types, e.g. Inscription.codec_scheme(::Type{MyType}, ::Endianness) = Inscription.BitsLoadable().\n\nUnlike the methods of a hand-written codec, its methods may be defined for abstract encoding types, such as Endianness or Encoding.\n\nSee also: CodecScheme\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.codec_scheme"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Inscription.codec_try_decode","page":"Reference","text":"codec_try_decode(input::DecodeInput, ::Type{T}, ::E)::Union{Tuple{T, Int}, TooShort, Invalid}\n\nExtension point of a hand-written codec for the value type T and the concrete encoding type E, see Encoding. Never call it directly; call e.g. try_decode. It is only needed for decoding: a codec that is only used for encoding need not implement it.\n\nDecode a T from the start of input, and return it with the number of bytes it was decoded from. Return an Invalid if the bytes are not a valid encoding. Its payload may tell why, and must be of the type codec_decode_invalid_type(T, E()), which defaults to Nothing, the type of Invalid().\n\nRead input only through require_bytes, peek_byte and decode_field, and return their TooShort with @?. The codec never handles a short input itself, see DecodeInput.\n\nAn implementation must follow this contract:\n\nPure. The result depends only on the bytes it read from input. The codec may be run more than once for the same bytes, e.g. again after returning TooShort, once more bytes are available.\nCounted. On success, the returned byte count is positive, and at most the number of bytes read. Bytes after the encoding that decide the result, e.g. a terminator, are read but not counted.\n\nFor example, for a 24-bit unsigned integer U24 encoded in 3 bytes:\n\nInscription.codec_max_encoded_size(::Type{U24}, ::BigEndian) = Val(3)\n\n@inline function Inscription.codec_try_decode(input::DecodeInput, ::Type{U24}, ::BigEndian)\n    b = @? require_bytes(input, 3)\n    # `b` has length 3, so these are in bounds, and their checks are removed\n    return (U24((UInt32(b[1]) << 16) | (UInt32(b[2]) << 8) | UInt32(b[3])), 3)\nend\n\nAn encoding whose end is decided by the byte after it looks at that byte with peek_byte. For example, for the decimal digits of a UInt8:\n\n@inline function Inscription.codec_try_decode(input::DecodeInput, ::Type{UInt8}, ::Decimal)\n    value = 0\n    n = 0\n    while n < 3\n        byte = @? peek_byte(input, n + 1)\n        (byte === nothing || !(UInt8('0') ≤ byte ≤ UInt8('9'))) && break\n        value = 10 * value + (byte - UInt8('0'))\n        n += 1\n    end\n    (n == 0 || value > 255) && return Invalid()\n    return (UInt8(value), n)\nend\n\nWhen only the bytes \"12\" are available, but the input may continue after them, peek_byte(input, 3) gets the third byte, which may be another digit, or returns TooShort if it is not available. When the input ends after \"12\", it returns nothing, and the result is 12.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.codec_try_decode"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Inscription.codec_try_encode!","page":"Reference","text":"codec_try_encode!(output::EncodeOutput, x::T, ::E)::Union{Int, TooShort, Invalid}\n\nExtension point of a hand-written codec for the value type T and the concrete encoding type E, see Encoding. Never call it directly; call try_encode! or encode!. It is only needed for encoding: a codec that is only used for decoding need not implement it.\n\nEncode x at the start of output, and return the number of bytes written. Return an Invalid if x can't be encoded. Its payload may tell why, and must be of the type codec_encode_invalid_type(T, E()), which defaults to Nothing, the type of Invalid().\n\nWrite output only through reserve_bytes and encode_field, and return their TooShort with @?. Don't read the bytes of output.\n\nAn implementation must follow this contract:\n\nOn success, the returned byte count n is positive, and the codec wrote no bytes after the first n. They may belong to the caller, e.g. when patching a field into an existing buffer, or be written concurrently by another task.\nWhen it returns TooShort or Invalid, it may have written to any byte of output. Since output never holds more than max_encoded_size(T, enc) bytes, these are at most the first max_encoded_size(T, enc) bytes of the caller's memory.\n\nFor example, for a 24-bit unsigned integer struct U24; x::UInt32; end encoded in 3 bytes:\n\n@inline function Inscription.codec_try_encode!(output::EncodeOutput, v::U24, ::BigEndian)\n    mem = @? reserve_bytes(output, 3)\n    mem[1] = (v.x >> 16) % UInt8\n    mem[2] = (v.x >> 8) % UInt8\n    mem[3] = v.x % UInt8\n    return 3\nend\n\nChecked rules\n\nThe rules are not checked, with one exception: an ErrorException is thrown if the returned byte count is not in 1:length(mem), also in an @inbounds context. This is because the byte count is used to consume bytes from writers, so a codec that broke its contract in this way would otherwise corrupt memory, or, by returning zero, make loops that decode or encode until the end of the data never end.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.codec_try_encode!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.decode-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MemoryView{UInt8}, Integer, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"decode(mem, [i::Integer,] ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::T\n\nLike try_decode, but throw the DecodeError instead of returning it. mem is converted to ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}.\n\nIn @inbounds context, the caller must guarantee that i ≥ 1, and that at the MemoryView has at least length length(mem) ≥ i - 1 + max_encoded_size(T, enc). If that is Unbounded, the caller must guarantee only that i is in 1:length(mem)+1.\n\nSee also: decode_next\n\nExamples\n\njulia> decode(MemoryView([0x01, 0x02, 0x03]), UInt16, BigEndian())\n0x0102\n\njulia> decode(MemoryView([0x01, 0x02, 0x03]), 2, UInt16, BigEndian())\n0x0203\n\njulia> decode(MemoryView(UInt8[]), UInt16, BigEndian())\nERROR: DecodeError: End of input: no bytes left to decode\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.decode"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.decode_all!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MutableMemoryView{T}, MemoryViews.MemoryView{UInt8}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"decode_all!(dst, src, enc::Encoding)::Tuple{Int, Int}\n\nDecode elements from the start of the bytes src into dst, until dst is full or the next element fails to decode. Return (n_elements_decoded, n_bytes_read).\n\nTo learn why decoding stopped before dst was full, call try_decode at the index after the bytes used. To throw that error instead, use decode_every!.\n\nThe arguments dst is converted to a MutableMemoryView{T}, and src to ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}. dst and src may not alias (overlap in memory).\n\nThe input src is treated as a complete input. That means that if a partial encoding is trailing in src, it will be attempted to be decoded, which may or may not fail. This means that if there is trailing data which is a prefix of an encoded element, and this prefix happens to decode on its own without error, it will be decoded. \n\nSee also: encode_all!, load_all!\n\nExamples\n\njulia> dst = zeros(UInt16, 3); src = [0x01, 0x00, 0x02, 0x00, 0x03];\n\njulia> decode_all!(dst, src, LittleEndian())\n(2, 4)\n\njulia> dst\n3-element Vector{UInt16}:\n 0x0001\n 0x0002\n 0x0000\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.decode_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.decode_every!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MutableMemoryView{T}, MemoryViews.MemoryView{UInt8}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"decode_every!(dst, src, enc::Encoding)::Int\n\nDecode elements from the start of the bytes src until dst is full, and return the number of bytes of src used. Bytes of src after those used are allowed, and are ignored.\n\nThe arguments dst is converted to a MutableMemoryView{T}, and src to ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}. dst and src may not alias (overlap in memory).\n\nIf an element fails to decode, throw its DecodeError, as decode throws it at the index where the element starts. So if src ends between two elements, the error is EndOfInput. Truncated means that src ended partway through an element. When decode_every! throws, the elements before the failing one have been written to the start of dst.\n\nSee also: encode_every!, load_every!\n\nExamples\n\njulia> dst = zeros(UInt16, 2);\n\njulia> decode_every!(dst, [0x01, 0x00, 0x02, 0x00, 0x03], LittleEndian()), dst\n(4, UInt16[0x0001, 0x0002])\n\njulia> decode_every!(dst, [0x01, 0x00, 0x02], LittleEndian())\nERROR: DecodeError: Truncated input: the input ended partway through an encoded value\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.decode_every!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.decode_exact-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MemoryView{UInt8}, Integer, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"decode_exact(mem, [i::Integer,] ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::T\n\nLike try_decode_exact, but throw the DecodeError instead of returning it. Supports @inbounds in the same way as decode.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> decode_exact(MemoryView(\"é\"), Char, UTF8())\n'é': Unicode U+00E9 (category Ll: Letter, lowercase)\n\njulia> decode_exact(MemoryView(\"éa\"), Char, UTF8())\nERROR: DecodeError: Trailing data: the input continues after the encoded value\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.decode_exact"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.decode_field-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{L}, Tuple{Inscription.MemoryInput{L}, Type{T}, Encoding}} where {L, T}","page":"Reference","text":"decode_field(input::DecodeInput, ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)\n\nDecode a T with the encoding enc from the start of input. Return (x, n, rest), where x::T was decoded from n bytes, and rest is the input after them. Else, return TooShort, or an Invalid whose payload is of the type decode_invalid_type(T, enc). Use with @? in codec_try_decode, for encodings that contain the encodings of other values.\n\nWhen the codec returns that Invalid, its own payload type must include that of T, see codec_decode_invalid_type. Otherwise, it may return its own Invalid instead.\n\nMay invalidate views of the input, see DecodeInput.\n\nWhen input is known to hold at least max_encoded_size(T, enc) bytes, the T is decoded on the fast path, and rest is known to hold the number of bytes that remain. So the fast path is known when the codec is compiled, for every field in turn.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.decode_field"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.decode_invalid_type-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"decode_invalid_type(::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Type\n\nThe type of the payloads of the errors of decoding a T with enc. When decoding a T fails, the error is a DecodeError{decode_invalid_type(T, enc)}, whose payload is Some(x), where x is the payload of the Invalid that decoding returned. The type is known when the caller is compiled.\n\nDo not add methods to this function. It is derived from the codec scheme of T, and from codec_decode_invalid_type:\n\nBitsLoadable: Union{}, since all bit patterns are valid.\nBitsStorable: the union of codec_decode_invalid_type(T, enc) and the decode_invalid_type of the types of its fields, if T is a struct.\nProxy{P}: the union of codec_decode_invalid_type(T, enc) and decode_invalid_type(P, enc).\nTuples: the union of the decode_invalid_type of their element types.\nManual: codec_decode_invalid_type(T, enc).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> Inscription.decode_invalid_type(UInt32, LittleEndian())\nUnion{}\n\njulia> Inscription.decode_invalid_type(Tuple{Bool, UInt8}, LittleEndian())\nNothing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.decode_invalid_type"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.decode_next-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MemoryView{UInt8}, Integer, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"decode_next(mem, i::Integer, ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Tuple{T, Int}\n\nLike try_decode_next, but throw the DecodeError instead of returning it. mem is converted to ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}.\n\nSupports @inbounds in the same way as decode.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> function decode_chars(mem::MemoryView{UInt8})\n           chars = Char[]\n           i = 1\n           while i ≤ length(mem)\n               (c, i) = decode_next(mem, i, Char, UTF8())\n               push!(chars, c)\n           end\n           return chars\n       end;\n\njulia> decode_chars(MemoryView(\"a€!\"))\n3-element Vector{Char}:\n 'a': ASCII/Unicode U+0061 (category Ll: Letter, lowercase)\n '€': Unicode U+20AC (category Sc: Symbol, currency)\n '!': ASCII/Unicode U+0021 (category Po: Punctuation, other)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.decode_next"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.encode!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MutableMemoryView{UInt8}, Integer, T, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"encode!(mem, [i::Integer,] x::T, enc::Encoding)::Int\n\nLike try_encode!, but throw instead of returning an EncodeError:\n\nWhen the bytes from i to the end of mem are too few, throw a LightBoundsError.\nWhen x can't be encoded, throw the EncodeError of kind InvalidValue.\n\nAs for try_encode!, if both apply, either may be thrown.\n\ni must be in 1:length(mem) -maxencodedsize(T, enc)+ 1.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> v = zeros(UInt8, 3);\n\njulia> encode!(MemoryView(v), 2, 0x0102, BigEndian()), v\n(2, UInt8[0x00, 0x01, 0x02])\n\njulia> encode!(MemoryView(v), 2, 0x01020304, BigEndian())\nERROR: LightBoundsErrors.LightBoundsError: out-of-bounds indexing: `collection[2:5]`, where:\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.encode!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.encode_all!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MutableMemoryView{UInt8}, MemoryViews.MemoryView{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"encode_all!(dst, src, enc::Encoding)::Tuple{Int, Int}\n\nEncode elements of src into the start of the bytes dst, until src is exhausted, dst has no room for the next element, or the next element can't be encoded. Return (n_elements_encoded, n_bytes_written)\n\nTo learn why encoding stopped before src was exhausted, call try_encode! at the index after the bytes written. To throw an error instead, use encode_every!.\n\nThe argument dst is converted to MutableMemoryView{UInt8}, and src to ImmutableMemoryView{T}. These two arrays may not alias (overlap in memory).\n\nIf encoding stopped before src was exhausted, up to maxencodedsize(T, enc) bytes after the first n_bytes_written, but not past the end of dst, may have been written, and their contents are unspecified.\n\nSee also: decode_all!, store_all\n\nExamples\n\njulia> dst = zeros(UInt8, 5);\n\njulia> encode_all!(MemoryView(dst), MemoryView(UInt16[1, 2, 3]), BigEndian())\n(2, 4)\n\njulia> dst\n5-element Vector{UInt8}:\n 0x00\n 0x01\n 0x00\n 0x02\n 0x00\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.encode_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.encode_every!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MutableMemoryView{UInt8}, MemoryViews.MemoryView{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"encode_every!(dst, src, enc::Encoding)::Int\n\nEncode every element of src into the start of the bytes dst, and return the number of bytes written. \n\nThe argument dst is converted to MutableMemoryView{UInt8}, and src to ImmutableMemoryView{T}. These two arrays may not alias (overlap in memory).\n\nIf an element can't be encoded, throw an exception:\n\nLightBoundsError if dst has no room for the element\nEncodeError of kind InvalidValue if it can't be encoded at all.\n\nIf encodeevery! throws, the elements before the failing one have been encoded into the start of dst. Then, the contents of up to [`maxencodedsize`(T, enc)](@ref maxencoded_size) bytes from where the failing element would start, but not past the end of dst, are unspecified.\n\nSee also: decode_every!, store_every\n\nExamples\n\njulia> dst = zeros(UInt8, 5);\n\njulia> encode_every!(dst, ['a', 'é'], UTF8()), dst\n(3, UInt8[0x61, 0xc3, 0xa9, 0x00, 0x00])\n\njulia> encode_every!(dst, ['a', 'é', '€'], UTF8())\nERROR: LightBoundsErrors.LightBoundsError: out-of-bounds indexing: `collection[4:6]`, where:\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.encode_every!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.encode_field-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{L}, Tuple{Inscription.EncodeOutput{L}, T, Encoding}} where {L, T}","page":"Reference","text":"encode_field(output::EncodeOutput, x::T, enc::Encoding)\n\nEncode x with the encoding enc at the start of output. Return (n, rest), where n is the number of bytes written, and rest is the output after them. Else, return TooShort, or an Invalid whose payload is of the type encode_invalid_type(T, enc). Use with @? in codec_try_encode!, for encodings that contain the encodings of other values. As for decode_field, a codec that returns that Invalid must declare its payload type.\n\nAs for decode_field, when output is known to hold at least max_encoded_size(T, enc) bytes, x is encoded on the fast path, and rest is known to hold the number of bytes that remain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.encode_field"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.encode_invalid_type-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"encode_invalid_type(::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Type\n\nThe type of the payloads of the errors of encoding a T with enc. When encoding a T fails, the error is an EncodeError{encode_invalid_type(T, enc)}, whose payload is Some(x), where x is the payload of the Invalid that encoding returned.\n\nDo not add methods to this function. Like decode_invalid_type, it is derived from the codec scheme of T, and from codec_encode_invalid_type:\n\nBitsLoadable and BitsStorable: Union{}, since every value can be encoded.\nProxy{P}: the union of codec_encode_invalid_type(T, enc) and encode_invalid_type(P, enc).\nTuples: the union of the encode_invalid_type of their element types.\nManual: codec_encode_invalid_type(T, enc).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.encode_invalid_type"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.is_padding_free-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"is_padding_free(T::Type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether the bits type T is guaranteed to have no padding on any platform. This is one of the requirements for T to be BitsLoadable or BitsStorable. Throw an ArgumentError if T is not a bits type, i.e. if isbitstype(T) is false.\n\nSince the alignment of types differs between platforms, T is checked against the strictest layout any platform could give it:\n\nA primitive type is aligned to its size. Primitive types must have a bit width that is a multiple of 8, and primitive fields of structs a bit width that is a power of two of at least 8.\nA struct is aligned to the alignment of its most aligned field.\n\nT is padding-free if each of its fields begins right where the previous one ends, its size is the sum of the sizes of its fields, and each of its fields is padding-free. Therefore, a type that has no padding on the current platform may still not be padding-free.\n\nThis function only depends on the layout of T, not on its codec_scheme. Calls with a constant T are constant-folded.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Unordered; a::UInt32; b::UInt64; end\n\njulia> struct Ordered; b::UInt64; a::UInt32; c::UInt32; end\n\njulia> Inscription.is_padding_free(Unordered)\nfalse\n\njulia> Inscription.is_padding_free(Ordered)\ntrue\n\njulia> Inscription.is_padding_free(Tuple{UInt16, Tuple{UInt8, UInt8}})\ntrue\n\nSee also: CodecScheme\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.is_padding_free"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.load-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufReader, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"load(io::AbstractBufReader, ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::T\n\nLike try_load, but throw the DecodeError instead of returning it.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = CursorReader([0x01, 0x02, 0x03]);\n\njulia> load(io, UInt16, BigEndian())\n0x0102\n\njulia> load(io, UInt16, BigEndian())\nERROR: DecodeError: Truncated input: the input ended partway through an encoded value\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.load"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.load_all!-Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufReader, MemoryViews.MutableMemoryView, Encoding}","page":"Reference","text":"load_all!(io::AbstractBufReader, dst, enc::Encoding)::Tuple{Int, Int}\n\nLoad elements from io into dst until dst is full, io is EOF, or the next element fails to load. Return (n_elements_loaded, n_bytes_consumed).\n\nThe argument dst is converted to a MutableMemoryView{UInt8}.\n\nElements are loaded all-or-nothing, as in load_into!. If load_into! stops before filling dst, you can use [try_load] to learn why loading failed.\n\nFor its buffering behaviour, see try_load.\n\nSee also: store_all, decode_all!, load_every!\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = CursorReader([0x01, 0x00, 0x02, 0x00, 0x03]);\n\njulia> dst = zeros(UInt16, 4);\n\njulia> load_all!(io, MemoryView(dst), LittleEndian()), dst\n((2, 4), UInt16[0x0001, 0x0002, 0x0000, 0x0000])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.load_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.load_every!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufReader, MemoryViews.MutableMemoryView{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"load_every!(io::AbstractBufReader, dst, enc::Encoding)::Int\n\nLoad elements from io until dst is full, and return the number of bytes consumed. The argument dst is converted to a MutableMemoryView{T}.\n\nIf an element fails to load, throw a DecodeError.\n\nThe elements are loaded all-or-nothing. So when load_every! throws, the elements before the failing one have been consumed from io and written to the start of dst, and nothing of the failing element has been consumed.\n\nFor the DecodeErrorKind of the thrown DecodeError, and for buffering behaviour, see try_load. From its throwing behaviour follows that if io ends between two elements, the error is EndOfInput, even when some elements were loaded.\n\nSee also: load_all!, load, decode_every!\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = CursorReader([0x00, 0x01, 0x00, 0x02, 0x00]);\n\njulia> dst = zeros(UInt16, 2);\n\njulia> load_every!(io, dst, BigEndian()), dst\n(4, UInt16[0x0001, 0x0002])\n\njulia> load_every!(io, dst, BigEndian())\nERROR: DecodeError: Truncated input: the input ended partway through an encoded value\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.load_every!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.load_exact-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufReader, Integer, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"load_exact(io::AbstractBufReader, nbytes::Integer, ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::T\n\nLike try_load_exact, but throw the DecodeError instead of returning it.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> load_exact(CursorReader([0x00, 0x01]), 2, UInt16, BigEndian())\n0x0001\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.load_exact"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.load_into!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufReader, MemoryViews.MutableMemoryView{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"load_into!(io::AbstractBufReader, dst, enc::Encoding)::Tuple{Int, Int}\n\nLoad elements from io into the start of dst. Return `(nelementsloaded, nbytesconsumed)\n\nThe argument dst is converted to a MutableMemoryView{T}.\n\nLoad at least one element, unless dst is empty, io is EOF, or the next element in io fails to load. Other than that, the number of elements depends on the internal buffer size of io. Therefore, this function may choose a buffer size more optimal for io than load_all! or load_every!.\n\nEach element is loaded all-or-nothing, as in try_load: loading stops at the first element that fails, and nothing of that element is consumed. So a following try_load(io, T, enc) reports why.\n\nFor its buffering behaviour, see try_load.\n\nSee also: load_all!, load_every!, store_into\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = CursorReader([0x00, 0x01, 0x00, 0x02, 0x00]);\n\njulia> dst = zeros(UInt16, 3);\n\njulia> load_into!(io, MemoryView(dst), BigEndian()), dst\n((2, 4), UInt16[0x0001, 0x0002, 0x0000])\n\njulia> load_into!(io, MemoryView(dst), BigEndian())\n(0, 0)\n\njulia> try_load(io, UInt16, BigEndian())\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.Truncated)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.load_into!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.max_encoded_size-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"max_encoded_size(::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Union{Int, Unbounded}\n\nUpper bound on the number of bytes of any encoding of a value of type T with enc, or Unbounded() if there is none. Which of them is known when the caller is compiled. Throw an ArgumentError if there is no codec for T with enc.\n\nDo not add methods to this function. Implement a codec instead, see Encoding.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> max_encoded_size(UInt32, LittleEndian())\n4\n\njulia> max_encoded_size(Char, UTF8())\n4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.max_encoded_size"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.peek_byte-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Inscription.MemoryInput{L}, Integer}} where L","page":"Reference","text":"peek_byte(input::DecodeInput, i::Integer)::Union{UInt8, Nothing, TooShort}\n\nGet the byte at index i of input, or nothing if the input ends before it. i must be positive. Use with @? in codec_try_decode.\n\nIf byte i is not available yet, return TooShort, see DecodeInput. So nothing always means that the input ends before byte i, and not that its bytes are not available yet.\n\nMay invalidate views of the input, see DecodeInput.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.peek_byte"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.require_bytes-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Inscription.MemoryInput{L}, Integer}} where L","page":"Reference","text":"require_bytes(input::DecodeInput, n::Integer)::Union{ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}, TooShort}\n\nGet the first n bytes of input, as a memory view of length n, or TooShort if the input has fewer bytes. Use with @? in codec_try_decode.\n\nWhen n is a constant of at most max_encoded_size, this can't fail on the fast path, and is compiled to no code. The view's length is then a constant, so indexing it with constant indices needs no @inbounds.\n\nThe view may be invalidated by later reads of the input, and this function may invalidate earlier views, see DecodeInput.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.require_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.reserve_bytes-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Inscription.EncodeOutput{L}, Integer}} where L","page":"Reference","text":"reserve_bytes(output::EncodeOutput, n::Integer)::Union{MutableMemoryView{UInt8}, TooShort}\n\nGet the first n bytes of output, as a mutable memory view of length n, for the codec to write, or TooShort if the output has fewer bytes. Use with @? in codec_try_encode!.\n\nAs for require_bytes, when n is a constant of at most max_encoded_size, this is compiled to no code on the fast path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.reserve_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.store-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufWriter, T, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"store(io::AbstractBufWriter, x, enc::Encoding)::Int\n\nLike try_store, but throw the EncodeError instead of returning it.\n\nSee also: encode!, store_every\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = VecWriter();\n\njulia> store(io, 0x0102, BigEndian()), store(io, 'é', UTF8())\n(2, 2)\n\njulia> io.vec\n4-element Vector{UInt8}:\n 0x01\n 0x02\n 0xc3\n 0xa9\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.store"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.store_all-Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufWriter, MemoryViews.MemoryView, Encoding}","page":"Reference","text":"store_all(io::AbstractBufWriter, src, enc::Encoding)::Tuple{Int, Int}\n\nStore elements of src into io until src is exhausted or the next element can't be encoded. Return (n_elements_stored, n_bytes_written).\n\nThe argument src is converted to an ImmutableMemoryView{T}.\n\nElements are stored all-or-nothing, so either a full element is stored, or else none of it is.\n\nIf store_all stops before exhausting src, you can learn why it stopped by calling try_store.\n\nFor buffering behaviour, see try_store.\n\nSee also: load_all!, encode_all!\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = VecWriter();\n\njulia> store_all(io, MemoryView(['a', 'é']), UTF8())\n(2, 3)\n\njulia> String(io.vec)\n\"aé\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.store_all"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.store_every-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufWriter, MemoryViews.MemoryView{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"store_every(io::AbstractBufWriter, src, enc::Encoding)::Int\n\nStore every element of src into io, and return the number of bytes written. The argument src is converted to an ImmutableMemoryView{T}. Throw an IOError if io cannot provide enough buffering, and an EncodeError if an element can't be encoded.\n\nFor details on the buffering behaviour, see try_store.\n\nEach element is stored all-or-nothing.\n\nSee also: store_all, store, encode_every!\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = VecWriter();\n\njulia> store_every(io, MemoryView(['a', 'é']), UTF8())\n3\n\njulia> String(io.vec)\n\"aé\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.store_every"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.store_into-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufWriter, MemoryViews.MemoryView{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"store_into(io::AbstractBufWriter, src, enc::Encoding)::Tuple{Int, Int}\n\nStore elements of src into io. Return (n_elements_stored, n_bytes_written) The argument src is converted to a MemoryView{T}.\n\nStore at least one element, unless src is empty or its first element can't be encoded. Each element is stored all-or-nothing: storing stops at the first element that can't be encoded, and nothing of that element is consumed. Use try_store of the failed element to find out why it couldn't be stored.\n\nFor buffering behaviour, see try_store.\n\nSee also: store_all, store_every, load_into!\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = VecWriter();\n\njulia> store_into(io, MemoryView(UInt16[1, 2]), BigEndian())\n(2, 4)\n\njulia> io.vec\n4-element Vector{UInt8}:\n 0x00\n 0x01\n 0x00\n 0x02\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.store_into"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.try_decode-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MemoryView{UInt8}, Integer, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"try_decode(mem, [i::Integer,] ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Union{T, DecodeError}\n\nDecode a T from mem, starting at index i (default: 1). Return the value, or a DecodeError. mem is converted to ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}.\n\nThe result is the same as decoding from mem[i:end], but without creating the slice. i must be in 1:length(mem)+1, or a LightBoundsError is thrown.\n\nTo also get the index after the encoding, e.g. for types whose encodings vary in size, use try_decode_next. Bytes after the encoding are ignored. To require that the encoding ends at the end of mem, use try_decode_exact.\n\nThe error kind is:\n\nEndOfInput if no bytes remain;\nTruncated if mem ends partway through an encoding;\nInvalidData if the bytes are not a valid encoding. Then the error may have a payload that tells why, see DecodeError.\n\nTo customize it for your own type, implement a codec, see Encoding, or add a method to it, see Overloading functions.\n\nSee also: decode, try_load\n\nExamples\n\njulia> try_decode(MemoryView([0x01, 0x02, 0x03]), UInt16, LittleEndian())\n0x0201\n\njulia> try_decode(MemoryView([0x01, 0x02, 0x03]), 2, UInt16, LittleEndian())\n0x0302\n\njulia> try_decode(MemoryView([0x01]), UInt16, LittleEndian())\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.Truncated)\n\njulia> try_decode(MemoryView([0x02]), Bool, LittleEndian())\nDecodeError(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.InvalidData)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.try_decode"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.try_decode_exact-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MemoryView{UInt8}, Integer, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"try_decode_exact(mem, [i::Integer,] ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Union{T, DecodeError}\n\nLike try_decode, but the bytes from i (default: 1) to the end of mem must be exactly one encoding of a T. If the encoding ends before the end of mem, return a DecodeError of kind TrailingData.\n\nThis function is useful when you need a priori information to determine when the encoding ends. For example, when decoding the decimal string \"12\" to a UInt8, this could be the concatenated encodings for 0x01, then 0x02, or the encoding of UInt8(12).\n\nSee also try_load_exact.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> try_decode_exact(MemoryView([0x01, 0x02]), UInt16, BigEndian())\n0x0102\n\njulia> try_decode_exact(MemoryView([0x01, 0x02, 0x03]), UInt16, BigEndian())\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.TrailingData)\n\njulia> try_decode_exact(MemoryView([0x01, 0x02, 0x03]), 2, UInt16, BigEndian())\n0x0203\n\njulia> try_decode_exact(MemoryView([0x01]), UInt16, BigEndian())\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.Truncated)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.try_decode_exact"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.try_decode_next-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{MemoryViews.MemoryView{UInt8}, Integer, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"try_decode_next(mem, i::Integer, ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Union{Tuple{T, Int}, DecodeError}\n\nLike try_decode, but return the value together with the index after its encoding, which is where the next value starts. mem is converted to ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}.\n\nThis is meant for decoding a sequence of values, in particular of types whose encodings vary in size, such as Char with UTF8. Like try_decode, it ignores @inbounds.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> mem = MemoryView(\"aé\");\n\njulia> try_decode_next(mem, 1, Char, UTF8())\n('a', 2)\n\njulia> try_decode_next(mem, 2, Char, UTF8())\n('é', 4)\n\njulia> try_decode_next(mem, 4, Char, UTF8())\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.EndOfInput)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.try_decode_next"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.try_encode!-Tuple{MemoryViews.MutableMemoryView{UInt8}, Integer, Any, Encoding}","page":"Reference","text":"try_encode!(mem, [i::Integer,] x::T, enc::Encoding)::Union{Int, EncodeError}\n\nEncode x into mem, starting at index i (default: 1). Return the number of bytes written, or an EncodeError. mem is converted to a MutableMemoryView{UInt8}.\n\nThe error kind is:\n\nInsufficientSpace if the bytes from i to the end of mem are too few for the encoding of x;\nInvalidValue if x can't be encoded with enc. Then the error may have a payload that tells why, see EncodeError.\n\nOn error, the bytes from i up to max_encoded_size(T, enc) bytes later, but not beyond the end of mem, may have been written, and their contents are unspecified. For InsufficientSpace, these are the bytes that a successful encoding would have covered.\n\nOn success, only the bytes of the encoding are written, no extra bytes.\n\nTo customize it for your own type, implement a codec, see Encoding, or add a method to it, see Overloading functions.\n\nSee also: encode!, store\n\nExamples\n\njulia> v = zeros(UInt8, 3);\n\njulia> try_encode!(MemoryView(v), 0x0102, BigEndian()), v\n(2, UInt8[0x01, 0x02, 0x00])\n\njulia> try_encode!(MemoryView(v), 2, 0x0304, BigEndian()), v\n(2, UInt8[0x01, 0x03, 0x04])\n\njulia> try_encode!(MemoryView(v), 0x01020304, BigEndian()) # too short\nEncodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds.InsufficientSpace)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.try_encode!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Inscription.try_from_proxy","page":"Reference","text":"try_from_proxy(::Type{T}, p::P, enc::E)::Union{T, Invalid} where {E <: Encoding}\n\nTry constructing a T from p::P, when codec_scheme(T, ::E) == Proxy{P}().\n\nThis method should be implemented only when the CodecScheme of (T, E) has been chosen as Proxy{P}.\n\nReturn an Invalid if p can't be converted to a T. Decoding a T then fails with InvalidData, even though the bytes are a valid encoding of a P. The type of its payload must be codec_decode_invalid_type(T, E()).\n\nIt is not called directly, but via the decoding or loading functions. It is only needed for decoding: a proxied type that is only encoded need not implement it.\n\nSee also: try_to_proxy\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.try_from_proxy"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.try_load-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufReader, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"try_load(io::AbstractBufReader, ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Union{T, DecodeError}\n\nLoad a value of type T from io, and consume its bytes. If the data does not hold a complete, valid encoding of a T, return a DecodeError.\n\nA load either consumes a whole encoding and returns a value, or throws and consumes nothing.\n\nSee also: load, try_decode, load_into!\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = CursorReader([0x01, 0x02, 0x03]);\n\njulia> try_load(io, UInt16, LittleEndian())\n0x0201\n\njulia> try_load(io, UInt16, LittleEndian()) # nothing is consumed\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.Truncated)\n\njulia> try_load(io, UInt8, LittleEndian())\n0x03\n\njulia> try_load(io, UInt8, LittleEndian())\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.EndOfInput)\n\nExtended help\n\nBuffering behaviour\n\nThis function calls grow_buffer until io is EOF, or enough bytes has been buffered in IO to decode the value, or to decide the value is invalid. By default, that is when max_encoded_size(T, enc) bytes are buffered. Unbounded types are buffered as their codec reads them. For variable-length encodings, like Char's UTF8 encoding, this can cause blocking. Suppose io contains the single byte 'a' (byte 0x61) and blocks until that is read. A char encodes in up to 4 bytes, so io would wait indefinitely for more bytes to arrive. But the codec of Char for UTF8 only reads the byte 0x61 to decode 'a', so io doesn't wait for more, see DecodeInput.\n\nToo short buffers\n\nIf io is not EOF, and its buffer contains fewer bytes than needed according to the section above, and its buffer cannot grow, throw an IOError of kind BufferTooShort. Therefore, you must ensure that a partial value is never in a non-growable buffer. To handle generic AbstractBufReaders, you can use BufferIO.expandable, which  guarantees this does not happen.\n\nError kinds\n\nThe DecodeErrorKind in the thrown error is given by this table\n\nSituation Result\nComplete, valid encoding available the value; its bytes are consumed\nEOF with no bytes left DecodeError(EndOfInput)\nEOF partway through an encoding DecodeError(Truncated)\nInvalid encoding DecodeError(InvalidData), with the payload of the codec's Invalid\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.try_load"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.try_load_exact-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufReader, Integer, Type{T}, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"try_load_exact(io::AbstractBufReader, nbytes::Integer, ::Type{T}, enc::Encoding)::Union{T, DecodeError}\n\nLoad a value of type T from the next nbytes bytes of io, which must be exactly one encoding of a T, as for try_decode_exact. On success, consume the nbytes bytes. Otherwise, return a DecodeError and consume nothing.\n\nUse it to load a value whose size is known from the data, such as a field with a length prefix, or a column of a fixed width. Unlike try_load, it waits for no more than nbytes bytes, and doesn't need the codec to find the end of the encoding.\n\nThe error kind is:\n\nEndOfInput if io is at EOF;\nTruncated if io reaches EOF before nbytes bytes;\notherwise, the error of try_decode_exact for the nbytes bytes. Since an encoding is at most max_encoded_size(T, enc) bytes, a larger nbytes always gives an error.\n\nThrow an ArgumentError if nbytes is negative, and an IOError of kind BufferTooShort if the buffer capacity of io is smaller than nbytes.\n\nSee also: load_exact\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = CursorReader(\"éab\");\n\njulia> try_load_exact(io, 2, Char, UTF8())\n'é': Unicode U+00E9 (category Ll: Letter, lowercase)\n\njulia> try_load_exact(io, 2, Char, UTF8()) # 'a' is followed by 'b'\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.TrailingData)\n\njulia> try_load_exact(io, 1, Char, UTF8()) # nothing was consumed\n'a': ASCII/Unicode U+0061 (category Ll: Letter, lowercase)\n\njulia> try_load_exact(io, 2, Char, UTF8())\nDecodeError{Union{}}(Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.Truncated)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.try_load_exact"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Inscription.try_store-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{BufferIO.AbstractBufWriter, T, Encoding}} where T","page":"Reference","text":"try_store(io::AbstractBufWriter, x, enc::Encoding)::Union{Int, EncodeError}\n\nStore x into io with the encoding enc, and return the number of bytes written. If x can't be encoded with enc, return an EncodeError of kind InvalidValue, and consume nothing.\n\nThrow an IOError of kind BufferTooShort if io can't provide a buffer of max_encoded_size(typeof(x), enc) bytes, or, for Unbounded types, of the size of the encoding, either because it is full, or because its buffer can't grow large enough. Writers that can always grow, such as those returned by BufferIO.expandable, never throw this. Like the buffer capacity of a reader for try_load, this is a property of how io was constructed, not of x, so it is thrown rather than returned. If try_store throws, nothing has been consumed.\n\nWhen x can't be encoded, bytes of the buffer of io after the consumed bytes may have been written, as by try_encode!. Since they are not consumed, they are not part of the output.\n\nSee also: store, try_encode!\n\nExamples\n\njulia> io = VecWriter();\n\njulia> try_store(io, 'é', UTF8())\n2\n\njulia> io.vec\n2-element Vector{UInt8}:\n 0xc3\n 0xa9\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.try_store"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Inscription.try_to_proxy","page":"Reference","text":"try_to_proxy(x::T, ::E)::Union{P, Invalid} where {E <: Encoding}\n\nTry constructing a P from x, when codec_scheme(T, ::E) == Proxy{P}().\n\nThis method should be implemented only when the CodecScheme of (T, E) has been chosen as Proxy{P}, see CodecScheme for more details.\n\nReturn an Invalid if x can't be represented as a P. Encoding x then fails with InvalidValue. The type of its payload must be codec_encode_invalid_type(T, E()).\n\nIt is not called directly, but via the encoding or storing functions. It is only needed for encoding: a proxied type that is only decoded need not implement it.\n\nSee also: try_from_proxy, CodecScheme\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.try_to_proxy"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Inscription.validate_bits","page":"Reference","text":"validate_bits(::Type{T}, bytes::NTuple{N, UInt8})::Union{Nothing, Invalid}\n\nCheck that the bytes bytes are a valid T, for a type T. Return nothing if they are, else an Invalid, whose payload may tell why. The type of the payload must be codec_decode_invalid_type(T, enc) for the Endianness encodings enc, which defaults to Nothing, the type of Invalid().\n\nThis function is only called for types whose codec_scheme is BitsStorable(). It is only called when decoding, so a type that is only encoded need not implement it.\n\nN is the number of bytes of T. The bytes are in given in native byte order, i.e. after any potential byte swapping. So, if bytes are valid, they are the raw bits of T, and the instance of T may be created with reinterpret(T, bytes). If T is a struct, the fields of T are checked before this is called, and the payloads of their errors are passed on, see decode_invalid_type.\n\nFor easier bit manipulation, you can use the helper function as_unsigned to convert bytes to an Unsigned integer in native byte order.\n\nExamples\n\nprimitive type Odd64 64 end\nInscription.codec_scheme(::Type{Odd64}, ::Endianness) = Inscription.BitsStorable()\nfunction Inscription.validate_bits(::Type{Odd64}, bytes::NTuple{8, UInt8})\n    return isodd(Inscription.as_unsigned(bytes)) ? nothing : Inscription.Invalid()\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.validate_bits"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/#Inscription.Little","page":"Reference","text":"module Little\n\nThe functions of Inscription, with the encoding fixed to LittleEndian(). Every function forwards to the function of the same name in Inscription, with LittleEndian() as the last argument.\n\nThe module also exports various other names, so that using Inscription.Little is all that is needed to use it.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Inscription.Little: load, store\n\nload(io, UInt32) # loads a little-endian UInt32\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.Little"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/#Inscription.Big","page":"Reference","text":"module Big\n\nThe functions of Inscription, with the encoding fixed to BigEndian(). Every function forwards to the function of the same name in Inscription, with BigEndian() as the last argument.\n\nThe module also exports various other names, so that using Inscription.Big is all that is needed to use it.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Inscription.Big: load, store\n\nload(io, UInt32) # loads a big-endian UInt32\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.Big"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/#Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds","page":"Reference","text":"module DecodeErrorKinds\n\nUsed as a namespace for DecodeErrorKind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.EndOfInput","page":"Reference","text":"EndOfInput\n\nDecodeError kind. See DecodeErrorKind and DecodeError for more details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.EndOfInput"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.InvalidData","page":"Reference","text":"InvalidData\n\nDecodeError kind. See DecodeErrorKind and DecodeError for more details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.InvalidData"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.TrailingData","page":"Reference","text":"TrailingData\n\nDecodeError kind. See DecodeErrorKind and DecodeError for more details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.TrailingData"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.Truncated","page":"Reference","text":"Truncated\n\nDecodeError kind. See DecodeErrorKind and DecodeError for more details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.Truncated"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.DecodeErrorKind","page":"Reference","text":"DecodeErrorKind\n\nEnum indicating why decoding or loading a value failed. The current list is non-exhaustive, and more may be added in future releases. The integral values of these enums are subject to change in minor versions, but its type is guaranteed UInt8.\n\nCurrent kinds:\n\nDecodeErrorKinds.EndOfInput\n\nThere were no bytes at all to decode from: a clean end of input.\n\nDecodeErrorKinds.Truncated\n\nThe input ended partway through an encoded value.\n\nDecodeErrorKinds.InvalidData\n\nThe bytes are not a valid encoding of a value of the requested type.\n\nDecodeErrorKinds.TrailingData\n\nA value was decoded, but the input continues after its encoding, where the input should hold exactly one encoded value. Only returned or thrown by the functions that decode exactly, such as try_decode_exact and try_load_exact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds.DecodeErrorKind"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/#Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds","page":"Reference","text":"module EncodeErrorKinds\n\nUsed as a namespace for EncodeErrorKind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds.InsufficientSpace","page":"Reference","text":"InsufficientSpace\n\nEncodeError kind. See EncodeErrorKind and EncodeError for more details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds.InsufficientSpace"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds.InvalidValue","page":"Reference","text":"InvalidValue\n\nEncodeError kind. See EncodeErrorKind and EncodeError for more details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds.InvalidValue"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds.EncodeErrorKind","page":"Reference","text":"EncodeErrorKind\n\nEnum indicating why encoding or storing a value failed. The current list is non-exhaustive, and more may be added in future releases. The integral values of these enums are subject to change in minor versions, but its type is guaranteed UInt8.\n\nCurrent kinds:\n\nEncodeErrorKinds.InsufficientSpace\n\nThe memory to encode into is too short for the encoding of the value. Nothing has been written. Only returned by try_encode!: encode! throws a LightBoundsError instead.\n\nEncodeErrorKinds.InvalidValue\n\nThe value can't be encoded with the encoding, for example a Char that is not in an 8-bit character set. Bytes up to the value's max_encoded_size may have been written. Never returned or thrown for types that are BitsLoadable or BitsStorable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Inscription.EncodeErrorKinds.EncodeErrorKind"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Implementing-a-codec-for-a-custom-type","page":"Implementing a type","text":"Instead of implementing optimized read and write methods for every combination of reading/writing function, I/O subtype, Encoding, and type, Inscription.jl abstracts this by asking you to implement one \"codec\" for each type/Encoding pair. Since most types only need one codec, this usually means one codec implementation per type to get a broad variety of encode, decode, load and store methods.","title":"Implementing a codec for a custom type"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Encodings-and-codecs","page":"Implementing a type","text":"A type may be written in several different ways. For example, 0xa53b may be written (in binary):\n\n3b a5 in little-endian binary\na5 3b in big-endian binary\n34 32 32 39 39 in ASCII decimal\n\n... and so on.\n\nThis is modeled as zero-sized Encoding subtypes. Users may define arbitrary Encodings and implement codecs for them.\n\nThis API consists of three methods. codec_max_encoded_size is always needed, while codec_try_decode is only needed for decoding, and codec_try_encode! only for encoding. So, if values of a type are only ever read, the codec need not implement codec_try_encode!, and vice versa. See their docstrings for details (click them to go to their docstring in this documentation).\n\nInscription.codec_max_encoded_size gives the maximal size in bytes for the encoding of any instance of a type, as a Val, or Inscription.Unbounded if there is none, as for strings.\nInscription.codec_try_decode attempts to decode an instance of T from a Inscription.DecodeInput.\nInscription.codec_try_encode! attempts to encode an instance of T into an Inscription.EncodeOutput.\n\nWhen the bytes are not a valid encoding, codec_try_decode returns an Invalid, and when a value can't be encoded, codec_try_encode! does. Its payload may say why, e.g. Invalid(Overflow), and is passed on in the DecodeError or EncodeError that the user gets. The payload types are declared with Inscription.codec_decode_invalid_type and Inscription.codec_encode_invalid_type. They default to Nothing, so a codec without payloads returns Invalid(). Like codec_try_decode and codec_try_encode!, the first is only used for decoding, and the second only for encoding.\n\ncodec_try_decode reads its input through Inscription.require_bytes, Inscription.peek_byte and Inscription.decode_field, and codec_try_encode! writes its output through Inscription.reserve_bytes and Inscription.encode_field. When loading from an AbstractBufReader, these make the reader wait for exactly the bytes the codec reads, so a codec for a variable-length encoding, like Char's UTF8 encoding, doesn't make loading wait for the maximal number of bytes of any instance of T. When the input is known to hold the maximal number of bytes, as it usually is, the checks of these functions for constant sizes are compiled away. Code only against the abstract DecodeInput and EncodeOutput: a codec is compiled for several internal input and output types, and must not depend on which one it gets. In particular, its input may not have all bytes available yet. Reads may make more bytes available, which may invalidate views from earlier reads, and the codec may be run again on the same bytes. See Inscription.DecodeInput.","title":"Encodings and codecs"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Deriving-codecs-automatically","page":"Implementing a type","text":"Instead of implementing a codec, a type can get one from its CodecScheme, by adding a method to Inscription.codec_scheme. It defaults to Manual(), which means the codec must be implemented manually, as described above.","title":"Deriving codecs automatically"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Bits-types","page":"Implementing a type","text":"Two encodings are special: LittleEndian and BigEndian (together: Endianness) represent the \"native\" binary representation of an object.\n\nNot all objects must have Endianness encodings. For example, the BigEndian encoding of the Julia Char 'Ø' would be c3 98 00 00 (same as reinterpret(UInt32, 'Ø') in big endian), but it may not be prudent to expose the internal binary representation of Julia objects. Instead, Inscription.jl defines a struct UTF8 <: Encoding end, and defines the encoding of 'Ø' under UTF8 as c3 98 (which is its representation in e.g. a Julia String).\n\nFor the endianness encodings, the codec may be derived from the bits of the type in memory:\n\nIf codec_scheme(T, e) returns BitsLoadable(), the type may be loaded and stored via pointer loads or stores, or memcpy.\nElse, if it returns BitsStorable(), it may be stored via pointer stores, but needs validation on loading, as not all bit patterns are valid.\n\nThis simplifies the common case for e.g. UInt32 where the endianness encoding is simply writing its bits directly to memory (possibly byte-swapping, depending on the host CPU endianness).","title":"Bits types"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Proxies","page":"Implementing a type","text":"A type that can be converted to and from another type P with a codec can be encoded exactly as P is, with any encoding that P has a codec for, by declaring codec_scheme to be Proxy{P}(). Then, implement Inscription.try_to_proxy, which is needed for encoding, and Inscription.try_from_proxy, which is needed for decoding. A type that is only encoded, or only decoded, only needs the one method. Both may return an Invalid: try_to_proxy if the value can't be represented by the proxy, which makes encoding fail with InvalidValue, and try_from_proxy if the proxy is not a valid value, which makes decoding fail with InvalidData. The errors of decoding and encoding the proxy are passed on, so the payloads of a proxied type are those of try_to_proxy or try_from_proxy, and those of the proxy.\n\nFor example, this struct has padding in memory, so it can't be BitsLoadable, but it can be encoded as a tuple, which has no padding in its encoding:\n\nusing Inscription: Inscription, Proxy, Endianness\n\nstruct Record\n    kind::UInt8\n    id::UInt32\nend\n\nInscription.codec_scheme(::Type{Record}, ::Endianness) = Proxy{Tuple{UInt8, UInt32}}()\nInscription.try_to_proxy(x::Record, ::Endianness) = (x.kind, x.id)\nfunction Inscription.try_from_proxy(::Type{Record}, (kind, id)::Tuple{UInt8, UInt32}, ::Endianness)\n    return Record(kind, id)\nend\n\nThe encoding of a Record is then 5 bytes: the kind, followed by the id. A proxy compiles to the same code as its proxy type does, except that the bulk functions, such as decode_all!, can't copy the memory of many values at once, as they can for BitsLoadable types.\n\nIf no other way applies, the codec has to be manually implemented, as described above. That is always a valid option, also for types that are not bitstypes, and for types whose encodings have no maximal size.","title":"Proxies"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#overloading","page":"Implementing a type","text":"A codec gives a type all the functions of Inscription.jl, and it can be nested in tuples, proxies and other codecs. But it isn't always the fastest option. For example, a codec reads a string that is terminated by some byte with Inscription.peek_byte, one byte at a time, where e.g. BufferIO.buffer_until is much faster for long strings.\n\nIn such cases, you may add methods for your type to the functions below, implemented directly with BufferIO and MemoryViews. There are two cases:\n\nThe type has a codec. Then the method is an optimization, and must behave exactly like the function would without it. The other functions may or may not use the method.\nThe type has no codec. Then only the function itself, and the functions that the table below says use it, work for the type. Adding methods to try_decode_next, try_encode!, try_load and try_store is enough for all functions that aren't bulk functions. The others throw an ArgumentError. Values of the type can't be nested in tuples, proxies or the codecs of other types.","title":"Overloading functions"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Which-functions-use-which","page":"Implementing a type","text":"Method added to Also used by Function that reports errors\ntry_decode try_decode without an index \ntry_decode_next Without a codec: try_decode, decode, decode_next, try_decode_exact, decode_exact, try_load_exact, load_exact \ntry_decode_exact try_decode_exact without an index, try_load_exact, load_exact \ndecode decode without an index \ndecode_next  \ndecode_exact decode_exact without an index \ntry_encode! try_encode! without an index. Without a codec: encode! \nencode! encode! without an index \ntry_load load \ntry_load_exact load_exact \ntry_store Without a codec: store \nstore  \ndecode_all! decode_every! try_decode_next\nencode_all! encode_every!, and store_into of types with a codec try_encode!\nload_into! load_all!, load_every! try_load\nstore_into store_all, store_every try_store\n\nThe functions that end in _every call the function in the last column to get the error of the element that failed. Without a codec, also add a method to that, or they throw an ArgumentError instead of the error. Functions with arguments that are converted, such as a Vector{UInt8}, and the functions in the modules Inscription.Little and Inscription.Big, use the method of the function of the same name. With a codec, decode, encode! and store do not use the methods of the try_ functions.","title":"Which functions use which"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Signatures","page":"Implementing a type","text":"To avoid method ambiguities and methods that are not called for some arguments:\n\nLet the types of the memory be MemoryView{UInt8} for bytes that are only read, and MutableMemoryView{UInt8} for bytes that are written. Likewise, let the elements be MemoryView{MyType} and MutableMemoryView{MyType}. Do not leave the argument untyped.\nLet the type of indices and byte counts be Integer, not Int.\nLet the type of IOs be AbstractBufReader or AbstractBufWriter.\nDefine the methods for a concrete type and a concrete encoding, at least one of which is defined in your package.\n\nFor example, for strings that are terminated by a zero byte:\n\nusing Inscription, BufferIO\nusing Inscription.DecodeErrorKinds: EndOfInput, Truncated\n\nstruct CStr\n    s::String\nend\n\nfunction Inscription.try_load(io::AbstractBufReader, ::Type{CStr}, ::LittleEndian)\n    buffer = buffer_until(io, 0x00)\n    # The buffer can't grow enough\n    buffer === nothing && throw(IOError(IOErrorKinds.BufferTooShort))\n    isempty(buffer) && return DecodeError(EndOfInput)\n    last(buffer) == 0x00 || return DecodeError(Truncated)\n    x = CStr(String(buffer[1:(end - 1)]))\n    consume(io, length(buffer))\n    return x\nend\n\nThis is enough for try_load and load to work.","title":"Signatures"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#What-the-methods-must-do","page":"Implementing a type","text":"Return what the docstring of the function says, also in the case of errors.\nLoad and store every element all-or-nothing: an element that fails consumes nothing from the IO. load_every! and the like call another function to get the error of that element, so if part of it was consumed, they may continue in the middle of the data.\nload_into!, store_into, decode_all! and encode_all! must return zero elements only if the destination or source array is empty, the IO is at EOF, or the next element fails. Otherwise, the functions that use them stop early.\nReturn the number of elements, and of bytes, that were actually processed. The element counts, and the byte counts where memory safety depends on them, are checked, and an ErrorException is thrown if they are out of range, but values in range are trusted.","title":"What the methods must do"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Visual-guide","page":"Implementing a type","text":"(Image: Guide to implementing a codec)","title":"Visual guide"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Structs","page":"Implementing a type","text":"To be BitsLoadable, all fields of the struct must be BitsLoadable. Similarly, to be BitsStorable, all fields must be BitsStorable or BitsLoadable (which implies BitsStorable).","title":"Structs"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Padding","page":"Implementing a type","text":"Padding is not allowed in derived codecs, because they present pitfalls for users. Because padding is platform-dependent, Inscription.jl uses a conservative algorithm for determining padding: It assumes the alignment of a primitive is equal to its size. A struct's alignment is the largest among its fields.\n\nSo, for example, this struct:\n\nstruct Foo\n    a::UInt32\n    b::Float16\nend\n\ncannot be BitsLoadable or BitsStorable, because it has sizeof(Foo) == 8, yet the sum of its field sizes is only 6. There are two bytes of padding at the end.\n\nTuples are special-cased structs, and allow padding. Padding is ignored when encoding and decoding, i.e. the fields are written in order, without padding.","title":"Padding"},{"category":"section","location":"custom/#Odd-sized-primitives","page":"Implementing a type","text":"As of Julia 1.14, Julia supports primitive types with a bit size not a multiple of 8, such as a 20-bit primitive type. These are known as odd-sized primitives (OSP). Because such types are stored with padding, OSP cannot be BitsLoadable or BitsStorable.\n\nFurthermore, the abstractions of Inscription.jl only have the concept of reading or writing an integer number of bytes. There is no interface to read 20 bits (2 bytes and half of the next byte).\n\nTherefore, any manual codec for OSP types must read an integer number of bytes, unpacking the bits by hand. This may be done by loading a wider type and casting to OSP, as in the example below. Note that in this case we still load an integer number of bytes, since 2 × 20 bits = 40 bits = 5 bytes.\n\nusing Core.Intrinsics: trunc_int\nusing BufferIO: AbstractBufReader, get_nonempty_buffer, consume\nusing Inscription: decode, LittleEndian\n\nprimitive type U20 20 end\n\nfunction load_two_u20(io::AbstractBufReader)\n    buffer = @something get_nonempty_buffer(io, 5) return nothing\n    a = decode(buffer, 1, UInt32, LittleEndian())\n    b = decode(buffer, 2, UInt32, LittleEndian())\n    consume(io, 5)\n    return (trunc_int(U20, a), trunc_int(U20, b >> 12))\nend","title":"Odd-sized primitives"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Reading-and-writing-simple-binary-values","page":"Examples","text":"This works similarly to Base's read and write (for readers), and unsafe_load, unsafe_store! (for memory), although these functions are not unsafe.\n\nusing BufferIO: CursorReader, VecWriter\nusing Inscription.Little # little endian encodings/decodings\n\nmem = collect(b\"abcdefgh\")\nrdr = CursorReader(mem)\nwtr = VecWriter()\n\n# Load a u32 from index 1 of mem\n# Can also use `decode_exact`, `decode_next`, or try_* versions\n# of each of these three - they have slightly different semantics.\nu32 = decode(mem, 1, UInt32)\n\n# Store that u32 back at index 5 of the same mem\n# Can also use `try_encode!` (again, slightly different semantics)\nencode!(mem, 5, u32)\n\n@assert mem == b\"abcdabcd\"\n\n# Load a u32 from the reader\n# Can also use `load_exact`, `try_load` and `try_load_exact`\nu32 = load(rdr, UInt32)\n@assert read(rdr) == b\"abcd\" # the rest, which `encode!` wrote above\n\n# Store that into the writer\n# Can also use `try_store`\nstore(wtr, u32)\n\nString(wtr.vec)\n\n# output\n\n\"abcd\"","title":"Reading and writing simple binary values"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Reading-simple-binary-values-in-bulk","page":"Examples","text":"This may be more efficient than the scalar operations above done in a loop.\n\nusing BufferIO: CursorReader, VecWriter\nusing Inscription.Little # little endian encodings/decodings\n\nu8s = rand(UInt8, 128)\nf32s = rand(Float32, 32)\nwtr = VecWriter()\nrdr = CursorReader(u8s)\n\n# Fill `f32s` with `Float32`s decoded from the bytes of `data`.\n# Can also use `decode_all!`\ndata = UInt8[i for i in 1:128]\ndecode_every!(f32s, data)\n@assert f32s == ltoh.(reinterpret(Float32, data))\n\n# Encode all the f32s into the u8s byte array\n# Can also use `encode_all!`\nencode_every!(u8s, f32s)\n@assert u8s == reinterpret(UInt8, htol.(f32s))\n\n# Fill f32s with data loaded from the reader\n# Could also use `load_into!` or `load_all!`\nload_every!(rdr, f32s)\n\n# Write data of f32s as byte encodings into writer\n# Could also use `store_into` or `store_all`\nstore_every(wtr, f32s)\n\n# output\n\n128","title":"Reading simple binary values in bulk"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Reading-a-composite-type","page":"Examples","text":"In this example, I read the top of a BGZF block header from an AbstractBufReader. This header consists of 8 integers of varying bit-widths, totaling 12 bytes in length. The example shows how I get a buffer of the required size once, then decode from the buffer with @inbounds, for maximal efficiency.\n\nusing BufferIO: AbstractBufReader, get_nonempty_buffer, consume, CursorReader\nusing Inscription.Little # little endian encodings/decodings\nusing MemoryViews: ImmutableMemoryView\n\nstruct Header\n    ID   ::NTuple{2, UInt8}\n    CM   ::UInt8\n    FLG  ::UInt8\n    MTIME::UInt32\n    XFL  ::UInt8\n    OS   ::UInt8\n    XLEN ::UInt16\nend\n\n# For example's sake, I don't do validation of individual fields here.\n# If they truly didn't need validation, the above struct would be\n# `BitsLoadable` (see below), and the implementation could be completely\n# automatically derived.\nfunction try_load_bgzip_header(io::AbstractBufReader)::Union{Header, Nothing}\n    # `get_nonempty_buffer` promises 12 bytes, or to return `nothing`. But, since\n    # `io` is generic, a user subtype may implement it incorrectly. So, I code\n    # defensively: Typeassert, and check the length afterwards.\n    # For a known AbstractBufReader type, these checks can be elided.\n    buffer = get_nonempty_buffer(io, 12)::Union{Nothing, ImmutableMemoryView{UInt8}}\n    buffer === nothing && return nothing # EOF before 12 bytes\n    checkbounds(buffer, 12)\n    # Call `decode` in an inbounds context, since we know we have 12 bytes now.\n    header = @inbounds begin\n        Header(\n            decode(buffer, 1, NTuple{2, UInt8}),\n            buffer[3], buffer[4],\n            decode(buffer, 5, UInt32),\n            buffer[9], buffer[10],\n            decode(buffer, 11, UInt16),\n        )\n    end\n    consume(io, 12)\n    return header\nend\n\nrdr = CursorReader(collect(0x00:0xff))\nheader = try_load_bgzip_header(rdr)\n\n# output\n\nHeader((0x00, 0x01), 0x02, 0x03, 0x07060504, 0x08, 0x09, 0x0b0a)","title":"Reading a composite type"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Reading-and-writing-custom-types","page":"Examples","text":"Inscription.jl requires you to opt in to reading or writing a struct. In the following examples, I'll demonstrate how to do so. I'll begin with structs that are easy to read/write, and each example will choose a type that has more complications when reading/writing than the previous one.","title":"Reading and writing custom types"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Custom-struct-1:-Entirely-transparent-(BitsLoadable)","page":"Examples","text":"This type has no padding, all bit patterns are valid, and its encoding is the same as its constituent bytes. So, it can be read and written via pointer operations. We can make Inscription use those by opting into the BitsLoadable trait. This will automatically make all reading and writing methods work.\n\nusing Inscription.Little\nusing BufferIO: CursorReader\n\nstruct Point2D\n    x::Float64\n    y::Float64\nend\n\n# Just opt into this trait\nfunction Inscription.codec_scheme(::Type{Point2D}, ::Inscription.Endianness)\n    Inscription.BitsLoadable()\nend\n\n# Now all writer and reader functions work for big endian and little endian\n# encodings\nrdr = CursorReader(zeros(UInt8, 50))\npoint = load(rdr, Point2D)\n\n# output\n\nPoint2D(0.0, 0.0)","title":"Custom struct 1: Entirely transparent (BitsLoadable)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Custom-struct-2:-Transparent,-but-need-validation-(BitsStorable)","page":"Examples","text":"This type has no padding, and its encoding is the same as its constituent bytes. However, not all bit patterns are valid. That means it can be read and written via pointer ops, but read instances must be validated. We can do that by opting into the BitsStorable trait, and implementing the validation function validate_bits, which returns nothing for valid bytes, and Invalid() otherwise:\n\nusing Inscription.Little\nusing BufferIO: CursorReader\n\nstruct EvenInteger\n    x::Int64\n\n    EvenInteger(x::Int64) = isodd(x) ? throw(ArgumentError(\"nope!\")) : new(x)\nend\n\nfunction Inscription.codec_scheme(::Type{EvenInteger}, ::Inscription.Endianness)\n    Inscription.BitsStorable()\nend\n\nfunction Inscription.validate_bits(::Type{EvenInteger}, bytes::NTuple{8, UInt8})\n    # Validate the bytes are those of an even integer. Here, I choose to use\n    # the helper function as_unsigned\n    u64 = Inscription.as_unsigned(bytes)\n    iseven(u64) ? nothing : Inscription.Invalid()\nend\n\n# Now all writer and reader functions work for big endian and little endian\n# encodings\nrdr = CursorReader(b\"44444444444444444\")\neven = load(rdr, EvenInteger)\n\n# output\n\nEvenInteger(3761688987579986996)","title":"Custom struct 2: Transparent, but need validation (BitsStorable)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Custom-struct-3:-Forward-to-an-existing-encoding-(Proxy)","page":"Examples","text":"This type has padding and so cannot be pointer-loadable. However, we can declare it can be read and written like an equivalent Tuple (which is special-cased by Inscription.jl to ignore padding). We implement the Proxy codec scheme and convert to/from that tuple.\n\nAn odd-sized primitive, such as a 17-bit integer, could use the same mechanism: Load a 32-bit integer, and check the unused 15 bits are zeroed out in try_from_proxy.\n\nusing Inscription.Little\nusing BufferIO: CursorReader\n\nstruct Padded\n    a::UInt64\n    b::UInt8\n    # N.B: 7 bytes of padding\nend\n\nfunction Inscription.codec_scheme(::Type{Padded}, ::Inscription.Endianness)\n    Inscription.Proxy{Tuple{UInt64, UInt8}}()\nend\n\nInscription.try_to_proxy(x::Padded, ::Inscription.Endianness) = (x.a, x.b)\n\nfunction Inscription.try_from_proxy(\n        ::Type{Padded},\n        x::Tuple{UInt64, UInt8},\n        ::Inscription.Endianness\n    )\n    Padded(x[1], x[2])\nend\n\n# Now all writer and reader functions work for big endian and little endian\n# encodings\nrdr = CursorReader(b\"123456789\")\npadded = load(rdr, Padded)\n\n# output\n\nPadded(0x3837363534333231, 0x39)","title":"Custom struct 3: Forward to an existing encoding (Proxy)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Custom-struct-4:-Manual-codec,-fixed-length","page":"Examples","text":"Here, I implement an encoding of UInt32 that prints it as lowercase hexadecimal digits, left-padded with zeros to 8 characters. Note that while UInt32 in little/big endian encoding is pointer-readable/writable, its hexadecimal encoding obviously isn't, so this can be neither BitsLoadable nor BitsStorable. Also, there is no equivalent encoding, so it can't be a Proxy.\n\nNote that this example needs to define a new Encoding, and can't simply be LittleEndian, as that means storing UInt32 as binary data.\n\nusing Inscription: Inscription, Encoding, decode, encode!, @?\n\nstruct LowerCaseHexadecimal <: Encoding end\n\nfunction try_hex_digit(byte::UInt8)\n    if in(byte, 0x30:0x39)\n        return byte - 0x30\n    elseif in(byte, 0x61:0x66)\n        return byte - 0x57\n    else\n        return nothing\n    end\nend\n\nfunction to_hex_digit(u::UInt32)\n    (u < 0x0a ? u + 0x30 : u + 0x57) % UInt8\nend\n\n# `codec_scheme(UInt32, ::LowerCaseHexadecimal)` defaults to `Manual`,\n# and needs three methods to make reading and writing work; implemented below\n\nfunction Inscription.codec_max_encoded_size(::Type{UInt32}, ::LowerCaseHexadecimal)\n    Val{8}() # Always 8 bytes, hence that's its max\nend\n\nfunction Inscription.codec_try_decode(\n        # This type wraps the input memory, or the input reader\n        input::Inscription.DecodeInput,\n        ::Type{UInt32},\n        ::LowerCaseHexadecimal\n    )\n    # Request exactly 8 bytes with `require_bytes`.\n    # If the input ends before that, it returns `TooShort()`.\n    # The `@? x` macro expands to `return x` if x is an error value like\n    # `TooShort` or `Invalid`.\n    # So, the line below means: Get me 8 bytes, or return `TooShort()` from\n    # this function if not available. The latter signals that we need more\n    # bytes to decode a UInt32 in hexadecimal\n    bytes = @? Inscription.require_bytes(input, 8)\n    # 8 hexadecimal digits are 32 bits, so this can't overflow\n    result = UInt32(0)\n    # `@inbounds` is safe here because `require_bytes` guarantees exactly\n    # 8 bytes.\n    @inbounds for i in 1:8\n        byte = bytes[i]\n        digit = @something try_hex_digit(byte) return Inscription.Invalid()\n        result = (result << 4) | digit\n    end\n    # Return (value, n bytes read)\n    return (result, 8)\nend\n\nfunction Inscription.codec_try_encode!(\n        output::Inscription.EncodeOutput,\n        x::UInt32,\n        ::LowerCaseHexadecimal\n    )\n    # `reserve_bytes` is the same as `require_bytes` above, but for `EncodeOutput`\n    bytes = @? Inscription.reserve_bytes(output, 8)\n    @inbounds for i in 8:-1:1\n        (x, r) = divrem(x, UInt32(16))\n        bytes[i] = to_hex_digit(r)\n    end\n    return 8 # return n bytes written\nend\n\nmem = zeros(UInt8, 8)\nencode!(mem, 0x0123abcf, LowerCaseHexadecimal())\n(String(copy(mem)), decode(mem, UInt32, LowerCaseHexadecimal()))\n\n# output\n\n(\"0123abcf\", 0x0123abcf)","title":"Custom struct 4: Manual codec, fixed length"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Custom-struct-5:-Manual-codec,-variable-length,-with-error-payloads","page":"Examples","text":"Here, I write UInt32 in decimal and without left padding with zeros. This adds two more complications compared to the example above:\n\nWhen decoding, the length can be arbitrarily long, since there can be any number of leading zeros\nEven when encoding, the output length is variable.\n\nDecoding can fail for two reasons: the number may not fit in a UInt32, or there may be no digits. The codec tells them apart with the payload of the Invalid it returns, so that a caller can e.g. try again with a wider integer type. The type of the payloads is declared with codec_decode_invalid_type, and the payload of the DecodeError is Some(Overflow) or Some(NoDigits).\n\nusing Inscription: Inscription, Encoding, DecodeError, load, store, try_load, @?\nusing BufferIO: CursorReader, VecWriter\n\nstruct ASCIIDecimal <: Encoding end\n\n@enum ParseError::UInt8 NoDigits Overflow\n\nInscription.codec_decode_invalid_type(::Type{UInt32}, ::ASCIIDecimal) = ParseError\n\nfunction Inscription.codec_max_encoded_size(::Type{UInt32}, ::ASCIIDecimal)\n    # If the encoding had disallowed leading zeros, this could have been\n    # Val{10}(), the maximal length of UInt32 in decimal.\n    Inscription.Unbounded()\nend\n\nfunction Inscription.codec_try_decode(\n        input::Inscription.DecodeInput,\n        ::Type{UInt32},\n        ::ASCIIDecimal\n    )\n    value = UInt64(0)\n    n_bytes_read = 0\n    while true\n        # `peek_byte` loads a byte, or returns `nothing` if the input ends\n        # before it. When loading from a reader, it buffers more bytes if\n        # needed. It can also return `TooShort()`, which the `@?` macro\n        # returns early, if the bytes are only the start of the input.\n        byte = @? Inscription.peek_byte(input, n_bytes_read + 1)\n        # Stop at the end of the input, or the first byte that isn't a digit\n        (byte !== nothing && in(byte, 0x30:0x39)) || break\n        value = value * 10 + (byte - 0x30)\n        value > typemax(UInt32) && return Inscription.Invalid(Overflow)\n        n_bytes_read += 1\n    end\n    iszero(n_bytes_read) && return Inscription.Invalid(NoDigits)\n    return (value % UInt32, n_bytes_read)\nend\n\n# This is easier, since we always encode without leading zeros\nfunction Inscription.codec_try_encode!(\n        output::Inscription.EncodeOutput,\n        x::UInt32,\n        ::ASCIIDecimal\n    )\n    nd = ndigits(x)\n    mem = @? Inscription.reserve_bytes(output, nd)\n    for i in nd:-1:1\n        (x, r) = divrem(x, UInt32(10))\n        @inbounds mem[i] = (r + 0x30) % UInt8\n    end\n    return nd\nend\n\nwtr = VecWriter()\nstore(wtr, UInt32(123), ASCIIDecimal())\nrdr = CursorReader(\"00456 \")\nerr = try_load(CursorReader(\"4294967296\"), UInt32, ASCIIDecimal())\n(String(wtr.vec), load(rdr, UInt32, ASCIIDecimal()), err.payload)\n\n# output\n\n(\"123\", 0x000001c8, Some(Overflow))","title":"Custom struct 5: Manual codec, variable length, with error payloads"},{"category":"section","location":"#Inscription.jl","page":"Inscription","text":"Inscription.jl provides fast, zero-allocation abstractions for reading and writing values to byte arrays (via MemoryViews.jl) or to I/O (via BufferIO.jl).\n\nIt can be considered a more powerful version of Base's read(::IO, T), write(::IO, ::T), or a safer unsafe_load/unsafe_store!. Alternatively, it can be viewed as an extension that allows writing arbitrary values of any type into the buffers provided by BufferIO.jl.\n\nYou might want to use this package if you find yourself:\n\nUsing unsafe_load, unsafe_store! a lot,\nCare about how much write(::IO, ::Float32) allocates or the speed of print(::IO, ::Int32) is,\nFind yourself writing non-UInt8 data into Vector{UInt8}s for IO performance reasons.\n\nYou probably don't need this package if:\n\nMostly doing I/O by writing strings or raw arrays e.g. to log files,\nRead/write specific formats like JSON, in which case you should use a package specific to that format.\n\nThe major design goals of this package are:\n\nHigh performance. The abstractions of this package should be roughly on par with manual implementations using pointer operations and reinterpret. Users should rarely be discouraged from using Inscription abstractions due to performance.\nPresent a hard-to-misuse API. Inscription APIs avoid making important behavior implicit, such as platform endianness, platform-dependent struct padding, or performance traps due to slow fallback definitions. It attempts to guide users away from bugs.\n\nExplicit non-goals of this package are:\n\nA simple API. This package prefers semantically consistent, precise, efficient, and hard-to-misuse APIs over easy APIs.\nReading/writing any type automatically via introspection, like Serialization.jl does.\nReading/writing nested types like nested dicts, common in e.g. JSON, TOML and BSON formats.\nSupport for any specific format like JSON or CSV. Instead, this package is for generic I/O of individual values, and can be used to implement formats.","title":"Inscription.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-start","page":"Inscription","text":"Want to jump right in? See the examples on the Examples page.\nIf you are reading or writing common types like Float64, UInt32, etc., see the Usage page.\nTo implement reading or writing of your own custom type, see the Implementing a type page.","title":"Where to start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Comparison-to-other-packages","page":"Inscription","text":"","title":"Comparison to other packages"},{"category":"section","location":"#BufferIO.jl","page":"Inscription","text":"BufferIO.jl provides I/O abstractions that are byte-oriented, meaning that BufferIO.jl types read and write UInt8 or MemoryView{UInt8}. Reading and writing other types such as Float32 is intentionally left out of BufferIO.jl. Inscription.jl builds on BufferIO.jl and depends on it.","title":"BufferIO.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#JSON.jl,-TOML.jl,-BSON.jl,-etc.","page":"Inscription","text":"These packages implement specific formats, whereas Inscription.jl provides abstractions for binary I/O in general. Also, the formats of these packages support variable-length containers and nested types such as mappings and arrays, whereas Inscription.jl has no built-in encodings of these. Users can implement encodings of variable length for their own types.","title":"JSON.jl, TOML.jl, BSON.jl, etc."},{"category":"section","location":"#Serialization.jl","page":"Inscription","text":"Serialization.jl can read and write any Julia value, including recursive types, and types with references like Dict and Array, whereas Inscription.jl has no built-in encodings of these, and doesn't preserve references. Also, Serialization.jl functions work for all types without specifying how to implement them, whereas Inscription.jl requires users to specify how each type should be serialized and deserialized. The latter is what makes Inscription.jl format-agnostic. Inscription.jl is more fundamental, in the sense that it could be used to implement Serialization.jl.","title":"Serialization.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Limitations","page":"Inscription","text":"Inscription.jl currently has poor support for packed binary values, such as reading a 22-bit and a 44-bit struct from 64 bits in total. For now, the package assumes types are read or written as an integer number of bytes. Users can opt in to padding to e.g. read/write an 18-bit primitive value as 24 bits (3 whole bytes). See the Implementing a type page for details.","title":"Limitations"}]
}
